Again me. Terry says the 800 5/300 SRAMs are 12 ns access (classic 

async, I suppose) parts with 22 ns burst cycles - around 43 MHz bursts, 

or 1/7 of 21164-300 clock. I'll be boring again, but use of these RAMs 

even in the top-end model proves to me that DEC's 21171 chipset for the 

21164 does not support burst SRAM - a feature we see even in Taiwanese 

UMC Pentium notebook chipsets, by the way.


Hopefully DEC people are reading this stuff. Once more, the fastest 

processors in the world (and they'll keep that leadership at least for a 

year of two more, obviously) shouldn't be crippled with the slowest
 
caches and DRAMs - even SPECmarks will suffer, not to mention real-world 

apps. I still hope to see that dual-Alpha desktop with 2 x 2 MB of 100 

MHz burst SRAM and 64 MB of 128-bit burst EDO DRAM for $25K or so, maybe 

for the launch of 400 MHz 21164A before yearend (if the 0.35 micron fab 

is working OK).

