In article <3qa8o2$62n@raffles.technet.sg>, michael@technet.sg (michael) says:
>Hopefully DEC people are reading this stuff. Once more, the fastest 
>processors in the world (and they'll keep that leadership at least for a 
>year of two more, obviously) shouldn't be crippled with the slowest
>caches and DRAMs - even SPECmarks will suffer, not to mention real-world 
>apps. I still hope to see that dual-Alpha desktop with 2 x 2 MB of 100 
>MHz burst SRAM and 64 MB of 128-bit burst EDO DRAM for $25K or so, maybe 

Hear hear.  And perhaps a 4 x 21164 SMP tower for $40K.  Something cool
to have at your deskside...  But the memory has to be there to back
the processors.

I've always suspected that there's a latent volume market for hot boxes
for software developers -- And I don't believe that cost is as much
of an issue as price/performance -- After all, in other industries
(like construction, for instance) it's common to spend $20K+ on a
pickup truck.  It's a tool to get the job done and it's useful.

I think the key to accessing that market is by paying attention to
price/performance.  Most buyers (or at least everyone I've met) estimate
system prices by adding together the cost of the components.  

I suspect the average customer (I haven't taken a survey) would come up with 
a higher cost (and be more willing to buy...) if he/she added up the cost of 
all the components from $0 as opposed to subtracting the "value" of the 
advertised components from the advertised price.  (In both cases to determine 
how "good" a price was...)

Support open buying by separating out the cost of each component so customers 
can adjust their spending accordingly.

Don't overcharge on captive market items (e.g., anything bundled into overall 
system cost) -- It's not profitable as it creates resentment and sacrifices 
overall system sales.  

A cheap keyboard is $10.  A good keyboard is $50.  Make both available.  
Keyboard cost is independent of system cost, whether the system is $600 or 
$60,000.  Don't let component cost creep because other numbers are higher.

Use standard components where possible to take advantage of economies of 
scale.

Make each component price/performance-competitive, and not just with
other products in the same price range.

Play to win.  Price down the volume curve in order to build volume and
recoup costs.  If a component can't sustain sufficient volume to have 
a low enough price to be price/performance competitive, then the business 
model/design for that component probably needs a hard look.

For most customers, the processor(s) are the key component(s).  But 
they're also accessories.  The central component in a system is the
motherboard (and box if non-standard boxes are needed).  Focus attention
on its design.  Sell motherboards as systems -- Everything else is optional.
That way you can have $1K-15K Alpha Systems with other components added
based on need.

Relationship marketing and customer-specific discounts are great and all...
BUT, the buying process for new h/w takes a long time for most customers.
They need time to get comfortable with the prices and services and generally
to reduce the risk of buying into the unknown.  Acid theory test: Divide the
number of issues of _Computer_Shopper_ sold by the number of orders placed...
Publish realistic prices, and distribute this information widely.  Furthermore,
publish prices for options and upgrades.  Anytime somebody thinks "Gee, this
new 120 Mhz EDO Pentium motherboard is only X dollars, I wonder what I would
get with an Alpha system and what it would cost?", make sure they have the 
answer sitting somewhere in the office.  Either in a magazine or on the web.  
And not just brand name marketing.  Include details.  After all, mind share 
precedes market share, right?

Advertise components and prices to a wider audience, not just the target 
market.  Your average college CS student may not buy a $40,000 server.  But 
he/she may buy a CPU and motherboard and memory and replace the guts of 
his/her existing system. Or maybe advise someone who will buy a system...  Or 
buy a system in a couple years...  Or join an ISV and port... Or otherwise 
influence market perceptions...

I've always liked the alpha architecture and would like to see it do better
in the marketplace.

I'd also like to see something like the following (end of 3Q95):

4 x 400 Mhz 21164 Alpha + 4 x 4 MB cache        4*(1500+1000) = 10000
64 bit PCI motherboard w 256 bit wide mem                        5000
256 MB RAM                                                       7000
620 Mbps ATM card on 64 bit PCI slot                             1500
