About the ATM card... Buying an ATM switch is pretty expensive right now...
but point-to-point links would work really well for small clusters...

About the motherboard...  $4K on the motherboard/switching h/w (not
including cache) should be enough for something sweet.  256 bit wide
memory / burst EDO / synch. SRAM caches / split transaction buses /
multiple banks and switched connects etc.  Whatever is necessary to
sustain full bandwidth read/writes for a 21164.

Perhaps removing the system bus might help.  Add a router chip to each 
system bus interconnect (1 per CPU, 1 for I/O, and 1 per memory bank).
Make this chip be responsible for cache-coherency protocols and buffering.  
Now add interconnects between the router chips.  Either fully interconnect
the router chips, or build support for (pre-programmed or loadable) virtual
circuits.  Since the interconnects are point to point, they can be made
really fast.  (like 64 bits at as many Mhz as crazy h/w guys can make it
go...  Or perhaps configure each link's connect speed based on the length/
signal characteristics of each link)  Design the router chips with minimal 
latency in mind.  Design cost recovery for the chips should be pretty easy -- 
Assume perhaps a chip price of $400, with 8 chips in the above system design.  
The chips are scaleable and could be used in multiple system designs (Imagine 
an MPP built from this!), so the total volume would be very high.  

The resulting system performance would be incredible.  Processors
would access multiple banks of fast wide system memory through their 
own switching fabric, caching the results.

It'd be worth it to build the system just for the advertising value alone 
of the resulting SPEC numbers for the appropriate processors.  (I know
SPEC benches systems, not processors, but processors get sold on SPEC
results)  As for being able to actually use a system like that...

