	One can combine the horizontal sync, vertical sync, and green
signal of a pc's vga card via at least three simple circuits that I
am aware of. One can also drive fixed frequency monitors, if you are
either lucky, skilled, or enjoy a combination of the two and run
XFree86...--or wealthy...I forgot that.
	Some fixed frequency monitors can be driven in standard graphics
modes by vga cards, given that you adjust the monitor a bit (lucky).
[Ive done this with 19" apollo monitors and some hp monitors.....]
Most modern vga cards are programable to the extent that they could
be made to drive a monitor, but that requires register level programing
(skill). If you run XFree, you  should be able to create a mode, and
drive the monitor--of course, you have to run X, under linux/netbsd/freebsd
etc. on a x86 machine.
	Either way, you have to run windows, or some graphical mode
usually; some people have a mono herc monitor that they use for text,
as these cards can often coexist with a colour vga.
	There are, however people who make cards specially made to drive
these sorts of fixed frequency monitors; tho I think they are expensive.
If you got one of these, you could do text and graphics easily.

