In article <6g4u2i3240.fsf@hodgkin.mbi.ucla.edu>, arne@hodgkin.mbi.ucla.edu 
(Arne Elofsson) writes...
> 
>I'm in the process to bye a set of workstation mainly
>for running a few home made fortran and c-programs,
>so all I really care about is floating point performance.
[snip]
>would NT be a serious alternative ? What compilers exist ?
>How much slower/faster are the compilers ?

If you are serious about getting every Mflop of performance that you
can, Digital Unix currently has a more extensive performance tool set
(for example, om, atom, kprofile, uprofile).  But NT provides
respectable performance, and uses the same compiler back-end technology. 
The current FORTRAN on NT is v1.1-670-292A.  The current C is Visual C++
V2.0 (yes, C++ is the one with the highest performance C compiler).

For example, LINPACK 100 gives 46 Mflops on an AlphaStation 250 4/266
running NT, but 54 under Digital Unix (formerly known as DEC OSF/1). For
LINPACK 1000 (the version that permits vendors to go all out in fine
tuning), the differences are more striking: 102 vs. 193. Livermore Loops
is 28 vs 42.  Double-precision Whetstone is 224 vs 256. SLALOM Mflops is
78 vs. 96.

Hope this helps

    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com
     Speaking for myself, not Digital
     Presently using NT.



