In article <D9AJnK.Ltz@mv.mv.com>, Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote:
>As readers of this and other DEC Alpha oriented lists may have
>noticed,  I have found the main memory bandwidth of the Alphas
>disappointing all along.  But there is evidence that parts of DEC are
>getting the clue (Ref: Site's talk at CMU re: memory bandwidth).  

Actually, as I see it, Sites' talk (which he also gave here) reported
work done _after_ the development of the 21164.  However, he did say
(as you suggest) that the 21164 has a better match of memory bandwidth
to CPU power than does the 21064.

I rather gathered that the appalling memory bandwidth results he
reported for the 21064 were as much a surprise to him as to anyone
else.

>And your suggestion about dual processors is contradictory.  Adding a
>second processor would be adding another competitor for that main
>memory bandwidth, and would make every access to memory slower due to
>cache coheriency protocol overhead.

Sites' work was investigating the bandwidth of the CPU's pins; he
demonstrated that a sample "awful" application* could overwhelm the
21064's ability to transfer data across its own pins, regardless of
how fast the memory was (e.g., if the whole program it was running was
in BCache).  The balance is shifted by the 21164's better-structured
on-chip caches.

I don't actually have any problem with the performance of the Alphas
we have here (all 21064, not even 21064a): we're not really into
running things that would overwhelm the processor.  Sites' results
seemed actually to make life easier for me (I'm i/c PALcode for our
OS): I feel less embarrassed about my inability to optimise some of
the code sequences that our OS requires.

* Those who haven't heard the talk are invited to guess which software
  house this awful application came from (which is, in fact, unfair to
  the said software house, but ...  ;-)
