:And your suggestion about dual processors is contradictory.  Adding a
:second processor would be adding another competitor for that main memory
:bandwidth, and would make every access to memory slower due to
:cache coherency protocol overhead.

Not if they have big and fast external caches. Even with 'only' 2 MB per 
CPU, 90% (almost the worst case) of each CPU's memory requests should be 
satisfied in their external caches. It would be quite rare occassion on 
which the remaining 10% or less will collide on the external bus at the 
same time. If you use BurstEDO or Sync. DRAM for faster bursts out of 
main RAM, the probability of collision is even lower as each CPU would 
complete its main memory requests faster. Am I right? 

Look at our 'beloved' P6, for instance. With its 133 MHz 256 or 512 K 
internal second cache on a separate 64-bit bus, Intel claims you can fit 
up to 4 P6 CPUs on a shared 66 MHz 64-bit memory bus without saturating 
it. R10000 and PPC620 also have separate cache buses to minimize the main 
bus contention. Maybe the end-1996 21264 Alpha should also implement a 
similar feature.

N Novakovic



