In article <3qf0aj$f4j@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>, rf@cl.cam.ac.uk (Robin 
Fairbairns) writes...

>I rather gathered that the appalling memory bandwidth results he
>reported for the 21064 were as much a surprise to him as to anyone
>else.

I sat near the front when Dick gave the talk at Digital's chip development
engineering facility, and have worked with him on the issue of memory
bandwidth.  The *surprise* is that in commercial codes, the memory bandwidth
problem is for the I-stream (that is, instructions).  

It has been well known that RISC systems differ from vector supercomputers
in having comparatively poor bandwidth, and an entire set of practices have
grown up around optimizing D-stream (that is, data) access to get the best
from RISC chips.  For example, see "High Performance Computing", by Kevin
Dowd, published by O'Reilly & Associates, chapters 3 and 11.  See also the
very nice marketing materials developed by the folks at SGI, who give
examples of how RISC systems can give you supercomputer level performance if
you arrange your D-stream access to optimize cache usage.  And the point of
Digital's DXML libraries (and other math libraries from other vendors) is
largely to do the operations in ways that use the caches to best advantage
... which is how EV5 (21164) breaks 400 Mflops on double-precision matrix
multiply.

In short, D-stream bandwidth issues and improvement methods on RISC
processors have been well documented, and solutions applied in many places.

Dick's point was that crawling around underneath a commercial database and
watching what it does to an Alpha reveals that there is an enormous I-stream
bandwidth requirement, because commercial codes don't sit in tight loops and
plow through tons of data doing matrix multiplies (or similar operations). 
Where the technical codes may fit entirely in the I-cache, and you only have
to manage your D-stream accesses, the commercial codes are nasty on both
fronts.

Now, before anyone runs off to buy a vector supercomputer (or a competitor
to Alpha) to run SQL databases, I should hasten to point out that Dick was
studying one of the standard TPC benchmarks.  So you can look at the audited
TPC results (of which Digital is proud) to see that although I-stream access
should be improved, Alpha is nevertheless a performance leader by the
standards of current industry practice.

    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com
     Speaking for myself, not Digital



