In article <D9849G.tL@spdcc.com> dyer@spdcc.com (Steve Dyer) writes:
> Any system which supports a shared libc can handle this nicely with
> Hesiodized getpw*() calls.  I believe this is built-in to DECathena
> already.
Well, it is built into Ultrix, but not into DECathena. Also, John's
comments concerning svc.conf passwd = local,bind entries are valid
for Ultrix systems, only.
Besides Ultrix, DECathena supports DEC OSF/1 (now Digital Unix), 
SunOS 4.1.3, Solaris 2.3, AIX 3.2.5, HP-UX 9.x. Unfortunately, 
there is no Hesiodized shared libc support for these systems,
not even for DEC OSF/1. For OSF/1, a DCE-like SIA integration
might be better, anyways.
So, Leslie's comments concerning the su and rsh problems hit
the point for all non-Ultrix DECathena systems.
In a regular DECathena setup, users get added to /etc/passwd
during login by libathlogin library calls, they are removed
on logout, with all the effects Leslie mentioned. This normally causes
no problems in the one user / one workstation Athena approach.
Yes, the use of su or rsh to other machines might be 
a non-Athena way of thinking, but this is real life. There
are compute servers, there are dialup servers, and, yes, users
want to start an application with rsh from these servers without
the need to telnet in first.
There are certainly ways to circumvent this, and we use some of
them (e.g. populating a more-or-less complete passwd file from
moira thru a dcm update for some machines (dialup servers, compute
servers), using an athenaized rexecd and sending passwords in 
cleartext over the wire to get an entry to the passwd file and to
get your locker attached, ...)

