organization: S.P. Dyer Computer Consulting, Cambridge MA
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <3pqg6l$i3u@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>,
Leslie Giles <giles@fig.nacto.lkg.dec.com> wrote:
   >|> >c) Kerberos tickets are needed for all nfs file accesses - 8, 14
   >|> 
   >|> No, just at NFS mount time (i.e., "attach" time).
   >|> The tickets aren't used for NFS accesses.
   >
   >I'm fairly certain that you're wrong - tickets are needed for all
   >accesses otherwise how could they expire and suddenly stop access
   >to a previously accessible file?

Er, I *know* you're wrong, because I wrote the rpc.mountd code
to support this.  UID mappings on the NFS server have the same
lifetime as the original ticket presented at NFS mount time.
In fact, this isn't a necessity; when we first worked on this at
Athena, NFS UID mappings remained in place for the lifetime of a
login session, though with the timeout, behavior is somewhat more
"AFS-like" (I won't say if this is an improvement! :-)) and more
in line with other services which require Kerberos.

   >Sounds good.  My major problem is that I don't see the reason for going
   >with Athena in the first place, so I don't see the reason for lots of
   >extra effort to get things that you get for "free" without Athena.

It's best to think of Athena (and DECathena) as a computing system
which really only begins to make sense when you have many, many
workstations and servers which have to be maintained by a small
staff.

Some of the other nits you mention (i.e., ~user, etc.) aren't a problem
with the original design of Athena; they just weren't deemed important
enough to build in.  (I haven't seen DECathena in a little more than
two years, but they've done a lot of work on it.)

