While I do, in general, agree with you, it is well worth pointing out the
difference between setuid-root programs and setuid programs.  A
setuid-other-than-root is a great way to share information in a controlled
way.  There are however security problems even with non-root setuid programs,
although I would still maintain that there are areas where they are useful.
The point is that Athena makes it extremely difficult to write setuid
programs by adding incompatible extensions to the Unix permissions scheme.

