One of the selling points of Athena is that you can publicize the root
password - therefore a "typical" Athena installation would have everybody
knowing their own root password.  A typical non-Athena network (at least
the ones that I worked in) will have a secure root password.  Of course
even if you hide your fileservers in locked rooms a user can still get
root access on his/her own workstation if you give him a screwdriver and
enough time...

After my time with Athena I've developed Giles law of system security:
any system that is sufficiently secure is not sufficiently usable, and
any system that is sufficiently usable is not sufficiently secure.


