In article <D8xA28.L5n@fibronics.co.il>, ricky@fibronics.co.il (Ricardo Marek) writes:
>How can I configure my startup files ( .cshrc, .login, .profile, .tcshrc)
>in order to get arround with the differences between OS types. (setting paths,
>environment vars, aliases, etc..), having also the possibility of seting particular
>stuff for a certain machine/host/OS System?
>
>- Using `uname` is not the short way, because SunOS and Solaris have the same
>  identification.
>
>- Using tcsh $OSTYPE is not portable in machines that doesn't have tcsh..
>
>Any hint for startup files using SunOS 4.*, Solaris 2.*, Ultrix, HPUX, AIX
>with the account, but logging in in different machines?
>
>PLEASE ANSWER VIA E-MAIL To: ricky@fibronics.co.il

Here is some of the very first lines in my .cshrc that allows me to
deal with a similar problem. It is kludgey for sure, but it works for
me and I do not have the time to change it:

#!/bin/csh
#
# Set up some window environment variables:
#
        set osbase = `uname`
        if ( $osbase == "SunOS" ) then
                set osver = `uname -r`
                if ( $osver == "4.1.3_U1revB" || $osver == "4.1.3_U1" || $osver == "4.1.3C" ) then
                        set osver = "4.1.3"
                endif
                if ( $osver == "4.1.1" || $osver == "4.1.2" || $osver == "4.1.3" || $osver == "4.1.4" ) then
                        set architecture = `/usr/bin/arch`
                        set system = `/usr/bin/hostname`
                else if ( $osver == "5.0" || $osver == "5.1" || $osver == "5.2" || $osver == "5.3" || $osver == "5.4" ) then
                        set architecture = `/usr/ucb/arch`
                        set system = `/usr/ucb/hostname`
                else
                        echo "Unknown osver = $osver"
                        set system = "UNKNOWN"
                endif
        else if ( $osbase == "HP-UX" ) then
                set architecture = "hp"
                set system = `/bin/hostname`
        else if ( $osbase == "IRIX" ) then
                set architecture = "mips"
                set system = `uname -a | awk '{print $2}'`
        endif

And after this code runs, I use "osbase", "architecture" and "system"
later in the file to decide what to do (paths, environment vars, etc.).

For example:

#
# OpenWindows environment variable:
#
        if ( $osbase == "SunOS" ) then
                setenv OPENWINHOME /usr/openwin
                if (! $?CEPATH ) then
                           setenv CEPATH "$HOME/.cetables:/etc/cetables:$OPENWINHOME/lib/cetables"
                endif
                set winpath = ($OPENWINHOME/bin $OPENWINHOME/bin/xview $OPENWINHOME/demo)
        else
                set winpath = ()
        endif

Note that some of the above are long lines and may wrap funny in your
News reader.  Sorry about that.

