should do something such as check whether "uname -m" is "i386" and, if
so, use *that* as the architecture, and otherwise use "uname -p" -
unless, of course, SVR4s on some *other* platforms act like x86 SVR4
rather than other SVR4's.

Note that BSD/OS 1.1 doesn't support "uname -p", but has a "uname -m"
that produces "i386", so it'd fall into the same category:

	% uname -p
	uname: illegal option -- p
	usage: uname [-snrvma]
	% uname -m
	i386
	% uname -s
	BSD/386

(BSD/OS 1.x was called "BSD/386" as it was PC-only; 2.x may get a port
to some SPARC machines and possibly others, and may call itself "BSD/OS").

However, on at least one Linux system, "uname -m" appears to say what
*flavor* of x86 chip you have, rather than that you have a chip that
supports the 32-bit version of the x86 architecture by saying "i386", so
on Linux (on which, at least one Linux system, "uname -s" prints
"Linux"), you have to handle "i586" and probably "i486" and map them to
"i386" (unless you really give a damn about the tiny additions the 486
and Pentium made to the user-mode instruction set).

At times, I want to send the folks who did "uname" commands and
"uname()" calls on various flavors of Unix to the penal colony in
Kafka's "In the Penal Colony", and set the machine to carve "give
'uname' an option to print out the ABI/instruction set architecture"
into their backs....

(And, no, POSIX doesn't help.  1003.2-1992 says "uname -m" should "Write
the name of the hardware type on which the system is running to standard
output", but I don't see any mention of "hardware type" in the index, so
I suspect they don't define it - probably so no system would actually
have to fix its behavior, even if that renders "uname -m" not convenient
to use in, oh, say, a *PORTABLE STARTUP SCRIPT* such as the original
poster wanted.  1003.1b-1993 says pretty much the same thing for
"uname()".)



