Thanks Terry for the Alpha 800 5/300 stuff, however I'm still a bit

disappointed with its SPECmarks compared to the same CPU in Alpha 8400

server. So, the workstation model again uses a smaller amout of slow 30 to

50 MHz (probably) cache, and usual 70 ns page-mode DRAM on 128 or 256 bit

data path. I'm afraid I'm right - new HPs, despite their inferior CPUs,

might again win in some apps with their much faster caches and RAM. 


DEC's Hoffman comments about price/performance tradeoffs. Understandable, 

but even the 'stupid' $3000 100 MHz Pentiums use 66 MHz burst caches and 33 

MHz EDO DRAM (and 66 MHz BurstEDO PCs will be out before yearend - many 

P6 machines are expected to use these for full-speed bursts on P6 

external bus). If they can have that stuff, why is it a problem to 

implement faster 75 or 100 MHz caches and EDO main memory on $10K - $30K 

Alpha desktops? I know Alpha has huge on-chip caches, and the next one 

will be even better there, however I can't fit my 10+ MB image file or 3D 

model in that cache for sure - main memory should be reasonably fast 

as well, and EDO/BEDO stuff isn't supposed to be too expensive.


Finally, why not dual Alpha desktop (separate, not shared external cache, 

of course)? I know quad 21164 or 21164A Alpha can't fit on a desktop, but 

dual one might just be nice for multithreaded UNIX or NT apps - in Real3D 

raytracing I need as much speed as possible. Put 2 MB of 100 MHz burst cache

on each of them, and 128-bit 50 - 66 MHz BurstEDO DRAM together with one 

66 MHz 64-bit PCI 2.1 slot and four secondary 33 MHz 64-bit PCI slots. 

You might say it's too much, but be warned - even Taiwanese Intellites 

are preparing cheap yearend desktop mainboards with specs quite like 

this, except that there'll be a pair of 133 or 166 MHz P6 inside. DEC 

systems designers, shift into the fifth gear!




