In article <3q4mqs$3v7@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>,
Leslie Giles <giles@fig.nacto.lkg.dec.com> wrote:
>I also found out about "hesinfo hannigan passwd" recently.  It would be
>possible to modify shells to get this to work, assuming you have the
>source for the shells (probably means moving everybody away from csh to
>tcsh or ksh - not necessarily a bad thing) - but from a system perspective
>it would be even nicer to modify the kernel to get the appropriate entry
>points to work properly.  Then all shells that support ~ would work.

~username has never been built into the kernel on most UNIX systems.
It isn't even recognized in the plain vanilla /bin/sh.  There's just
special code to handle it in the various shells.

Any system which supports a shared libc can handle this nicely with
Hesiodized getpw*() calls.  I believe this is built-in to DECathena
already.

