Not so big, smaller than the 11/34a system that I used to have in the
spare bedroom. 11/750 is not so power hungry, can be run off a power point
in the study.  Must admit that I had the builder provide a 15A 240V socket
in the spare bedroom (study) for just this machine. 11/780 would be three
phase and much more expensive to wire. 

Memory is not the problem, disks are more difficult to find at a
reasonable price. Large capacity disks are still of value, large physical
disk (washing machines) might be cheap but are a pain at home. 

>>You're not being fair to this fine machine.  It I recall correctly,
>>it's repairable because it was built using lots of TTL chips, not any

They tend to be so cheap that you can afford to have a second or third 
system in the garage for board swapping. Avoids the problem that the 
circuit diagrams are useless without the gate array info distributed on 
microfiche which are virtually unavailable to the average user. Does have 
the advantage of UNIBUS I/O cards which seem to be throw away items these 
days.

>AFIK you don't recall correctly. The 11/750 is _stuffed_ with custom gate
>arrays, and is a right pain to repair. The only 2 vaxen I'd have at home are
>the 11/780 (and later versions) if I had room, and the 11/730 (which is  all 
>PALs, TTL, and a pair of custom chips - the memory ECC gate arrays). 

11/730 is slower again, probably memory limitations again, still sucks the
juice. Powering the 11/780 would be difficult at home and there is the
small matter of cooling. 

