|> >Marek) writes:
|> >>How can I configure my startup files ( .cshrc, .login, .profile, .tcshrc)
|> >>in order to get arround with the differences between OS types. (setting
|> >>paths,
|> >>environment vars, aliases, etc..), having also the possibility of seting
|> >>particular
|> >>stuff for a certain machine/host/OS System?
|> >>
|> >>- Using `uname` is not the short way, because SunOS and Solaris have the
|> >>same
|> >>  identification.
|> 
|> 
|> No they don't. Use `uname -a` and a simple awk script to get the 3rd field
|> of the output, which is 4.x on SunOS and 5.x on Solaris.
|> 
|> 
|> ------------------------------------------------------------
|> Ido Sarig			|  Phone: (+972) - 3 - 5388846
|> XRunner Project Manager		|  Fax:   (+972) - 3 - 5331617
|> Mercury Interactive Corp.	|
|> 				|  E-mail:	ido@mercury.co.il
|> 
Here is how I do it:
Note that I have had uname return two words on some machines.

case `uname | awk '{print $1}'` in
SunOS)  if [ -f /bin/arch ]
        then  ARCH=`arch`
        else ARCH=sun4v
        fi

Other machines may need a similar treatment for example:

ULTRIX) case `uname -m` in
        RISC) ARCH=decds;;
        VAX)  ARCH=vax;;
        *)    ARCH=deca;;
        esac

