In article <3pvkb8$s0q@raffles.technet.sg>, michael@technet.sg (michael)
writes:

:If I'm right, most current Alpha PCI desktops, incl. DEC 250 4/266 and 
:Aspen Alpine 275, don't really excel in cache and memory speeds. Even 
:Pentiums nowadays use burst SRAMs to get 66 MHz and faster bursts, and 
:EDO/BurstEDO DRAMs improve system RAM performance. Even 21171 chipset for 
:the bandwidth-hungry 21164 doesn't seem to include support for these new 
:memories.

    As you are well aware, off-chip access is a design trade-off
    between the system performance and the system price -- adding
    faster inter-chip connections adds to the overall system price.

    You may also want to determine what the characteristics and the
    speeds of the current design are -- I don't have this information
    handy, but I have seen references to folks erroneously quoting
    the PCI bus speed as the processor-to-memory interconnect speed.
    The EV5 hardware manual will have the maximum speed at which the
    EV5 can drive the tertiary cache.  (It would not surprise me to
    learn that this was upwards of 100 MHz -- but, as I mentioned, I
    don't have a copy of this manual handy to check.  EC-QAEQA-TE.)

    Both the EV4/EV4s (21064) and the EV5 designs incorporate on-chip
    caches, the EV5 (21164) design incorporates large on-chip caches.
    (96 kilobyte dedicated to the secondary cache, and eight kilobytes
    each for I & D primary caches.  All are on-chip.)  Both the EV4
    and the EV5 designs allow designers to select (trade-off) various
    speeds and sizes for the off-chip (backup/tertiary) cache...

    Both EV4 and EV5 supports off-chip (backup/tertiary) cache access
    speeds at fractions of the system clock -- this allows the system
    designers to choose a speed appropriate for the target price and
    performance.

    Versus the 100 MHz Pentium, the 300 MHz EV5 has triple the integer
    performance, and six times the floating point performance.

    I don't specifically know if the 21x7x chipsets will or will not
    work with SRAMs, and I'm not willing to speculate on future systems
    nor on new and/or improved chipsets.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
