In Article <25MAY199507025125@almach.caltech.edu>
shoppa@almach.caltech.edu (Timothy D. Shoppa) writes:
>Do a
>
>$BACKUP/IMAGE/IGNORE=INTERLOCK DUAx: DUAy:
>
>and you'll be set.  Except, possibly, if you're using a 11/725, 11/730,
>11/750, 11/780, or 11/785; I believe you may need to do a WRITEBOOT
>on these, even though it's been so long that I've forgotten exactly
>when this has to be done.  On some recent versions of VMS (I've forgotten
>about pre-version-4, and I don't know a thing about 6.x) a
>
>$MCR WRITEBOOT
>
>will do the WRITEBOOT.

This is close, but the 780 and 785 don't use WRITEBOOT and it's not REALLY
required for other systems.

The advantage of doing a WRITEBOOT on the small ( <=750 )systems is that they
boot using a TU58 tape which is S--L--O--W and it takes forever to do a normal
VMS bootstrap via this technique. To improve boot times they have a small boot
ROM that is normally used to boot, but it's not big enough to have VMB.EXE, so
the technique of writing the location of the on-disk VMB.EXE and a few other
things into the boot block of the disk is used.

More recent systems have a much bigger ROM and can do a full boot operation on
known devices without using the boot block as a shortcut.

