amazing post on this subject, I'd like to say:

   1) I got all your jokes (I think).  Some of them went waaay back!

   2) Your writing style was very impressive.  This was masterful sarcasm: 
      so well stated that the victims might not even realize how badly they
      were being needled, and stated without recourse to vulgar language or 
      assuming the worst motives on the part of the ones you needled.  Indeed,
      you make it clear that they believed they were Right.

   3) Underneath the jokes, history, and sarcasm there lay some important 
      points about human engineering (which at the risk of being pedantic 
      I'd like to make explicit):

        * The intent to provide logical foundations for human interfaces 
          may err by not recognizing that existing habits have a logic
          of their own, which does not match the designer's logic.

        * When an interface designer is most sure that satisfying the 
          customer's request would be Wrong, this is the time that it 
          is most important to stop and re-examine assumptions.

        * Just because you have one human interface breakthrough (the 
          LK201 placement of the arrow keys, which has been widely imitated) 
          doesn't mean you've got Everything Right.

The only quibble I have with your article is calling the ergonmics folks
"drones".  The LK201 designers did get the arrow keys right, and that
was indeed a breakthrough.

Attached below is Aaron Leonard's original article.  Thanks for writing
it.

    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com
     Speaking for myself, not Digital
     Typing on a Digital DECpc keyboard (PC7XL-AA), with "Esc" clearly 
        labelled, on a key of its own, in easy reach.


*** begin repost of Aaron Leonard's article:

In article <1995May19.190134.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu>, 
fairfield@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu writes:

( re DEC providing a real ESC key on their keyboards)

| [...]

|        VMS users don't need such a  thing,  but it doesn't get in their
|    way  (my  3rd-party terminal at home is like that), so why not  give
|    your customers what they want?  :-)

Because it would be Wrong.

The <ESC> sequence is, as all good ANSI-standards X3.64 committee
meeting attendees well know, a code that is intended only to be a prefix
to a function sequence, and not to be emitted, naked and appalling,
from a human fingertip.

The canons of human decency and good engineering practice militate
against making easily available to ordinary users the ability easily
to produce an ASCII code regarded as so repulsive in good committee-
meeting-attending society.  (Of course, us bit-grinders know that
CONTROL/[ sends an octal 33, always has, and always will, but
such practices are well beneath the threshhold of comprehension of 
those who dwell at the religious, or even ergonomic, layers.)

Having a key that transmits an octal 33 is Wrong, just as TCP/IP
on VMS is Wrong (K.O. '88), just as burdening a command-line parser
with command-completion and option display (VMS Engineering, '77 -
'95) would be Wrong.

I suspect, actually, that the demise of the <ESC> key - which after
all, was in full view on the seminal VT52 and VT100 keyboards - was
only exoterically due to the ANSI-fied character-code cleansing
drive of the late seventies.  More significant, I think, was the
fact that the extermination of the <ESC> at that time marked the
rewriting of ASCII history by the triumphant 32-bitters - not only
would they repudiate the heritage of their 36-bit forebears, but
they would even amputate the protuberance required by those hoary
progenitors to invoke their familiar TECO and COMND JSYS mantras.

Think about it: alone of the keycaps on the traditional keyboard,
the "escape" has no straightforward meaning or rendering - its role,
rather, is to provide an, er, "escape" from the all-too-plausible
interpretations of the other 101 keys.  (Contrast the "control" key,
whose putative power consists only in its ability to coerce other,
more prosaic keys, to do its brutal, but ultimately banal, bidding.)

In fine, I would submit that the mocking presence of such a seditious 
agent as the "escape" key, in a terrain otherwise all too meticulously 
groomed by the drones of ergonomics, was simply found too
incomprehensible to be tolerated.

Aaron

Aaron Leonard           MultiNet-VMS@Support.TGV.COM [ work ]        
                        Leonard@Leonard.Tucson.AZ.US [ home ]



