>No they don't. Use `uname -a` and a simple awk script to get the 3rd field
>of the output, which is 4.x on SunOS and 5.x on Solaris.

Use "uname -r" and *no* script, "awk" or otherwise, to get the version
number of the OS, which is 4.x in SunOS 4.x (including 4.1.x, the OS
component of Solaris 1.y) and 5.x in SunOS 5.x (the OS component of
Solaris 2.x).  "uname" can be told to print particular values; you're
not obliged to get it to print all its values and use an "awk" or "sed"
or whatever script to select one of them.




