}on Kerberized filesystems as though I was you.  How do you avoid this?

    And how is this *less* secure than normal NFS?

    BTW, our su doesn't have a well known password (that doesn't
    really add any security, just makes trivial attacks like
    you described less trivial).

    I don't see how you can ever make a multi-user system secure
    against someone who has or can obtain (through physical access,
    for example) "root" access to it.

    This is why client systems are intrinsically single-user
    under Athena -- if you violate this constraint you should
    understand the consequences.

