In article <1995May22.104513.6621@venus.gov.bc.ca>, ewilts@vmsmail.gov.bc.ca (Ed Wilts) writes:
> I don't believe it was the backplane but rather the translation module (the
> 0005 board?).  Didn't it need to be upgraded to a 0008?  Boy, that's taking me
> back a few years, and I was a software guy!

  It was the memory controller. I believe the latest is L0022 (part of the
MS750-HB kit). I thought the earlier one was L0014, but I might be mistaken.

  The 750 had lots of ECO's. DEC finally gave up and put a daughtercard on
the backplane so the system knew what revision it was. Of course, most of the
750's I've met had the switches on the daughercard set wrong...

  I believe all the address lines for 8MB were always on the backplane. To
get to 14MB you had to install 2 wire-wrap jumpers (one from the memory con-
troller to array slot 1, and one from array slot 1 to slot 2).

