|>>In article <RLM.95May14174153@netcom20.netcom.com>,
|>>Robert McMillin <rlm@helen.surfcty.com> wrote:
|>>>Not really.  DEC has far too many other failings: an irritating
|>>>tendency toward "customer control" measures like non-standard hardware
|>>>interfaces (try to plug in that Keytronics keyboard or serial mouse to
|>>>your Alpha some time!),
|>What if you want a trackball or touchpad. Does Dec have anything like this?
|>Dec Direct sure doesn't seem much help. I have a DecStation 3100 and
|>need something that is compatible.

Today's AlphaStations use PC-style "PS/2" connectors.  I went to CompUSA
and bought a Logitech TrackMan Vista for my AlphaStation 400 - just plugged it
in and it worked.  The keyboard interface is standard too, though as far as
I know only Digital sells the LK411-style 108-key keyboard.  

Yes, the older systems had non-standard interfaces, but so did most of the
industry - standardization on the PS/2 connector is very recent.  Even today
most of the mice and trackballs come in "serial" and "bus" versions.
