In article <JOHNSONMD%DFSEL.32@dfmail.usafa.af.mil>, JOHNSONMD%DFSEL@dfmail.usafa.af.mil (M. Douglas JOHNSON) writes:

>Can I add a non-DEC SCSI hard drive to a DECsystems 5000 model 240?

Probably.

>
>I have a Seagate Barracuda 4 (4.3 GB) SCSI hard drive I would like
>to add to my 5000/240.  (It's not what I requested, but it is what
>the contracting folks gave me as an adequate equivalent.)  If it
>should work, what steps do I follow to install it.  (I have an SZ12 
>expansion box & the cables seem to be compatible.)  I have Ultrix
>4.4: do I use 'creatediskbyname', 'rzdisk'???

If the Seagate is well behaved ULTRIX shouldn't have any trouble
using it.  And for what its worth, I don't recall seeing many
notes from people complaining of Seagates misbehaving.  ULTRIX
is limited to using 2 GB partitions, but that's easily solved
by partitioning the disk.

0.  Physical installation.  If the bracket for the SZ12 slot will
    securely fit around the disk, install it in the box.  You don't
    want the disk to be able to slide around inside the box.  Select
    a SCSI ID that isn't in use.  The system host adapter is either
    6 or 7 (probably 7).  After that just be sure not to reuse one.

    With the disk connected and the system turned on, use the console
    "cnfg" command to verify that all the devices are seen.  "cnfg"
    by itself will give the system overview and from that you can get
    the TURBOchannel number of the SCSI controller (3 I think).  Then
    use that to scan the bus:

	>>cnfg #

1.  Boot the system and watch the boot messages to verify that all
    the devices are seen.  Watch for the device name of the Seagate
    since you'll need that later.  It will be:

	rz#, where # is (8 * bus#) + target#

    The base system SCSI bus is bus 0, so it may just be the target
    ID.

2.  Create the special device files.  With the system up and logged
    in as root:

	# cd /dev
	# MAKEDEV rz#

3.  Create a small file system.  You'll likely write over this later,
    but since you'll have to repartition to make good use of the disk,
    you need this initial partition to get the partition table on the
    disk:

	# newfs /dev/rrz#a seagate

    Don't worry about disktab information.  ULTRIX can get what it
    needs from the driver.

4.  Plan and partition the disk.  ULTRIX has a limit of ~2 GB for
    disk devices.  The maximum partical limit for a device that is
    going to hold an 8 KB file system 4,194,288 sectors.  Use the
    chpt command to repartition.

5.  Create the new file systems, mount and go.

>
