Subject: Performance boost for DEC 2000 AXP?
Date: 1995/05/24
Message-ID: <3pvbrd$jnl@zippo.uwasa.fi>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 103237068
organization: University of Vaasa, Finland
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

This is sort of a PC-related article, but the machine in this case is more
of a Unix system than a PC.

I am a quite happy DEC 2000/300 aka AXP PC aka Jensen user for about two
years now. I have found out that some 2000/300 users are not so happy and
would like to have at least a little more performance. Usually upgrading
memory from 32 to 64 megs would be enough, or changing the QVision adapter
to for example Number 9GXE could do the trick.

I think AXP PC's are quite a few. If one thinks AXP PC to be somekind of
a PC, then it would be thinkable to make somekind of an upgrade besides
the memory, graphics adapter, or, getting one of the new AlphaStations.
(I took a look at the ADVANTAGE program at DEC's www-site.)

As I read Byte's comparison on the fastest NT machines, I found out that
Nekotech's machine which was the fastest in the tests uses a 275 Mhz chip
which has been overaccelerated to 289 Mhz with a custom crystal. As well as
that, the article stated that DEC engineers have tested selected 275 Mhz chips
with up to 330 Mhz. I started to wonder: could one just get a new crystal for 
the 2000/300 too?

Secondly, PC Magazine had also a comparison last summer. In a table
describing the specifications of the machines (i.e NeTPower, Carrera, AXP PC)
it read that the two Alpha machines, Carrera and AXP PC had TAZ-processor
upgrade path. This would stand for Tool-Activated ZIF. I contacted the local
DEC about this processor upgrade and they said that it is not supported and
had no other information. The Carrera machine had an 200 Mhz Alpha and
looked exactly similar to AXP PC. This lead me to think that processor up-
grade would be possible or Carrera would have a different motherboard that
could be installed into AXP PC.

I contacted Nekotech as well and they replied that the processor cannot
be upgraded or the motherboard changed. The motherboard in the 2000/300
is not of the baby-AT size so Nekotech did not have a solution. But, my
guess would be that a baby-AT motherboard could be installed into the
2000/300 chassis. It would need some drilling and possibly somekind of
rearranging to ensure proper airflow and cooling. Some OEM Alpha
manufacturers have mentioned this problem with their motherboards. 
Any comments?  

Our machine is leased so I do not want mess too much with my machine, but
I am at the same time writing an article "Jensen in memoriam" for the local
Alpha Generation. In it, I am going to try to take more out of the AXP PC.

So if anybody knows anything more about the following questions:

1) Is it possible to upgrade the 150 Mhz 21064 chip in the 2000/300?
2) Could the 150 Mhz chip be overaccelerated with a faster crystal? (I know,
   a wild guess)
3) Does any OEM manufacturer have a solution (faster chip, faster motherboard)
   for the AXP PC? 
4) Does anybody have it already?
5) Any DEC representative, what would you recommend for a customer who wants
   more performance from a 2000/300? 

I _can_ take a "No" for an answer. But if there is a solution, then I would
think someone else other than me might be interested here in Finland.

TIA,
Pete.



