>machine/host/OS System?
>
>- Using `uname` is not the short way, because SunOS and Solaris have
>the same identification.

This is not true.  For me, "uname -r" will return "5.4" for Solaris, and
"4.1.3" for SunOS, "A.09.04" for HP-UX.

You can have a .profile thus:

   TCSH=/usr/local/bin/tcsh
   if test -x $TCSH ; then exec $TCSH ; fi


You can put something like this in your .cshrc, in case your chsh is
csh because not all the machines you log into have tcsh:

   if ( $?term ) then
      set TCSH = /usr/local/bin/tcsh
      if ( ! $?MYSHELL && -x $TCSH) then
         setenv MYSHELL $TCSH
         exec $TCSH -l
      endif
   endif
   set whatos = `uname -r | sed 's/\..*//'`
   if ( "$whatos" == 5 ) then
      # Solaris
   else if ( "$whatos" == 4 ) then
      # SunOS or Ultrix
      endif
   else
      # HP-UX
   endif


YMMV

hth,
