In article <1995May21.120215.118@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se>,
Anders Ostling <anos@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se> wrote:
>What options are there if we want to serve our VMS file systems to WFWG
>clients runnint TCP/IP ? Note that we would prefer NOT to install NFS
>clients on the PC's, but rather use LANMAN functionality on the servers...
>
>We do have Samba on AIX/Linux and would prefer something like that on the
>VMS hosts too.

Well, you could port Samba to VMS and get the world's gratitude.
Or, you could run Pathworks on the VMS box.
Or, you could run NFS on the VMS box, export to the AIX and Linux boxes,
and have them serve to the PCs via Samba (yuck!).

>Then again, if there are a good NFS client that can co-exist with MS TCP/IP
>and has full data-transparency against Unix and VMS file types, then I can
>change my mind ;-).

Why use MS TCP/IP? You get what you pay for...  most NFS clients for
the PC come with much better TCP stacks... I'd recommend Frontier
SuperTCP (also availible as Intercon TCP/Connect II for Windows), but
FTP, Beame and Whiteside, and other vendors products will get the job
done. As for full data transparency, unless you can convince the PC to
understand a Real Filesystem (ie versioning and record-oriented) then
you lose :-).

I have heard rumors that there is a free NFS client for the MS
(Wolverine VxD) TCP stack, but I do not have a name or pointer
handy.

--andrew
