|> >a) The passwd database on each workstation is not maintained by
|> >Hesiod (the Athena counterpart of NIS) - 3, 5, 10, 13, 17
|> 
|> This is a problem with OSF/1, not Athena.

I don't understand why you're saying this.  Hesiod maintains the passwd
database information in Kerberos.  NIS maintains the passwd database in
NIS/NFS systems.  NIS distributes the passwd information; Hesiod doesn't.
Unix doesn't have anything much to do with this kind of activity - it
maintains a single passwd database on the local system only.

|> >b) Kerberos tickets are associated with a user id and not with a
|> >login session - 7, 9
|> 
|> Not on our systems, they aren't.

In Unix the concept of a login session is very ill-defined.  For example
with regard to tickets: 1) your tickets will last beyond your login session;
2) anybody else with the same uid on the same system can use your tickets;
3) tickets can expire before your login session is completed.  If you don't
have these problems then I'm very interested in how you did it.

|> >c) Kerberos tickets are needed for all nfs file accesses - 8, 14
|> 
|> A definite plus.
|> 
|> 
|> >d) Kerberos tickets expire after a period of time - 12
|> 
