Not if you're running a server process or a long batch job - is security
so important that it should stop you getting your job done?

|> >e) Mail is implemented in a completely different way - 6
|> 
|> So, who cares?   Besides, if you don't like it, change it back.

You could say the same thing for every component of Athena, and I'd
jump at the opportunity.

|> >f) Filesystems need to be explicitly "mounted" or attached - 4
|> 
|> Big deal.  With 20,000 users, how else are you supposed to do it?

What's wrong with the automounter?

|> >g) Much of the Athena system is driven by user-visible settings, e.g.
|> >environment variables and Athena-specific versions of standard Unix
|> >tools - 11
|> 
|> A definite plus.  The user controls his envoronment, not the administrator
|> of each of a 1,000 different machines.

You can administer those machines centrally using NIS.

Really, I am interested in how you avoid some of the problems that Athena
introduces - especially the passwd file stuff.

