In article <3pi6mv$6bp@data.interserv.net>, levine@amarex.com writes:
:I have two Openvms 6.1 Alpha AXP systems connected over thin-line ether
:also on this ether are three vax/vms 5.1-5.5 machines all machines
:communicate perfectly and at the expected speeds _EXCEPT_ the two
:Alpha AXP 6.1 machines??? Both AXP machines where default configured
:with netconfig ( I did not create all the default user account though ). 
:When I "set host" or copy over the network the speeds between these two
:machines ( the alpha open vms 6.1 AXP machines , that is) is at best
:close to 2400 baud ( yes ) all other machine to machine communication
:through decnet is great JUST THE 6.1 AXPs have trouble.

  I'd look around for Ethernet cable faults -- such as under- or
  over-terminated ThinWire segments.  I'd particularly concentrate
  on the segment on which the OpenVMS Alpha systems are connected,
  but I would review the entire local network given that there are
  five nodes present.

  One should look for errors in the line and circuit counters in NCP,
  and one should physically review the Ethernet cabling.  (You do not
  mention the network configuration or devices in use, nor do you
  indicate if the three OpenVMS VAX systems are ThinWire, `FatWire',
  twisted pair, etc.)

  (In this case, I'd try cabling the two systems together on a completely
  isolated ThinWire Ethernet network to see if it performs as expected.
  To do this, I would obtain two 50 ohm terminators, two T-connectors,
  and a hunk of ThinWire cabling.  If this runs correctly, I'd then look
  at the connection between the ThinWire and the other nodes -- does this
  connection make the ThinWire too long, is there a fault in the repeater,
  brouter, transciver, or bridge, etc.)


:    ANY thoughts ??? This is a very simple decnet NO routing ( at least
:none that I know of ) 

  There is always some form of routing present, even if it's of the end
  node cannot see a designated router and performs the `routing of last
  resort' -- `toss the packet out onto the Ethernet and see if somebody
  answers'.  Most networks have one or two DECnet routers present, even
  if it's just for the cluster alias.  (If you eventually start looking
  to _add_ a router, I'd look at adding a brouter or other dedicated
  device, and I'd look at adding one that could route DECnet, IP and OSI
  protocols.)

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
