In article <3pq0f7$56u@fstgds15.tu-graz.ac.at> Thomas Leitner,
tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at writes:
>So how I connect that disk to my configuration? Apparently from the
>two cables between the RA81 and the terminator box, only one cable

To check the RA-82 for a locked head you need to lift up the hood so to
speak - the same way as on the RA-81. lift the top of the drive by
reaching under the front plate where there is some room and lifting up.
You should after you lift the top (it is spring and shock-absorber loaded
kind of like your hood of the automobile check to see if the lock is in
the LOCK or UNLOCK position. You should see the big round HDA (where the
actual drive is) and the lock should be toward the front of the drive
right in the middle of it.

Usually, if the lock is ON, the drive will try to spin up and then light
the fault light.

Make sure all the indicator lamps are working by turning on the drive and
leaving it in the standby mode (don't press the run/stop button) depress
the fault button and hold it in - check to see that all lamps on the
front of the drive light up. If ones do not, you may have burned out
lamps. It doesn't affect the operation of the drive but it is a little
anoying sometimes... If the RUN/STOP lamp is burned out, you won't be
able to tell if it is indicating the drive attempting to spin up or not. 

Finally, the RA82 will spin up whether it has a connection on it or
not... so for now, don't worry about whether you are connected to a
system or not...

If the drive still does not spin up, you may need to check the fuses in
the power supply - the motor should start to turn and you should hear it
- it is as loud as the old 81's. If it doesn't, check fuses (the easy
stuff) first. If you still need help, please feel free to e-mail me - 

mike_st@iah.com



