>c) Kerberos tickets are needed for all nfs file accesses - 8, 14

No, just at NFS mount time (i.e., "attach" time).
The tickets aren't used for NFS accesses.

>f) Filesystems need to be explicitly "mounted" or attached - 4

I investigated Kerberizing the automounter; it didn't look all that hard;
in fact, I had a prototype working with Athena Kerberized NFS servers.
The big issue had to do with the fact that Athena as distributed
doesn't use NIS (arguably a win), so there'd have to be some degree
of higher-level architectural work to integrate the automounter into
an Athena installation.  Not a big deal.

I don't know if I'd say that "Athena isn't UNIX"; it reflects one set
of engineering choices made to satisfy the needs of a widely distributed
computing environment ca. 1985 or so.  Undoubtedly if it were designed
today for the first time, it might look somewhat different.

