Below are a couple of postmortem-type messages from comp.arch (I
think) on the subject of Multiflow.  You may find them informative,
and may be able to get a contact address or two.  Also, I think that
Yale had a Multiflow user's group; you might ask postmaster@yale.edu
if they have any archives of their old local yale.multiflow-users
newsgroup.  And then you might want to try shaking the trees in
comp.arch to see what falls out.

------ Forwarded Messages

Date:    7 Oct 91 09:03:51
From:    colwell@pdx023.intel.com (Robert Colwell)
Subject: Re: 10@ Multiflow [1]

In article <4584@ssc-bee.ssc-vax.boeing.com> carroll@ssc-vax (Jeff Carroll) wri
tes:

> I think that part of the Multiflow problem is that they sent their
> sales force out with inadequate technical support. They're not the
> only vendor ever to have this problem (one or two of the Big Boys come
> to mind), but the unconventional nature of their product demanded
> people capable of understanding and explaining it.

Multiflow certainly did have a marketing/sales problem. In fact, it had
more than one.

At the beginning, MFCI tried to position itself as a purveyor of
technology; "hey, this VLIW stuff is great, it's different, it's high-tech,
look-ma-no-vectors, it's glitzy, it's the future, your code will love you
for it, you need this." No matter WHAT you build, you'll sell a few at the
beginning, and maybe enough to make it look like your sales projections
aren't made of vapor.

But then we discovered that we were making no dent at all in any sales
situations other than against vector machines, and here we were trumpeting
our ability to run unvectorizable code well. What did they care, their code
was already vectorized.  Definite impedance mismatch, and not overlooked by
the customers. We ran vector code very well, but that wasn't the message we
were so energetically trying to get across, so the prospect always seemed
to approach MFCI somewhat pre-confused.

The "unconventional nature of our product" probably scared off way more
people than it ever attracted. Too much money was at stake for anyone to
want to just "try it out"; they were betting their jobs that it would work,
and here we were extolling its weirdness. Again, our message didn't line up
properly.

Our original marketing director soon got the firing he so desperately
needed, and we tried to back off of that early positioning. Our sales force
began seeking out the vector prospects, but our marketing campaign didn't
reflect that focus, whether because they thought it wouldn't help, or too
much corporate pride was at stake, I don't know.

We were also noticing that for every sale we'd make (or lose, for that
matter), DEC had sold a dozen new VAXes, each with a much higher sales
price than we would have gotten. So then we tried to go after some of
those, but here FUD reigns supreme, since the guys who make the buy
decisions aren't the ones who use the machines, and are much less likely to
risk their jobs on a startup.

Ultimately we discovered that VLIWs ran FFTs extremely well (especially
when you used Paul Rodman's hand-coded FFT) and we sold several machines
into that market. But that market is way too small to float a systems
startup.

So yes, Jeff, we made the marketing mistake you're pointing out, but we
made others too. In fact, I wonder if we didn't inadvertently span the
marketing-error space over the 6 years of MFCI's existence.

