What version of ULTRIX are you running?  (I've seen missleading messages in old
versions of doconfig.)  Did you follow the exlpicit directions in the system
administrator's volumes to save the old kernel?  If so, I suggest you boot the
old kernel, restore the old kernel, reboot, and try again.  If you saved the
old kernel in /, you can specify it as the file in the boot command.  (see more
below)

>
>When I rebooted The system started properly until it got down to the bind
>section - but since I changed the hostname and all that, it says invalid
>address - cannot assign, etc. but I would expect it to error as I have
>not got the domain name server referred tp
>I get to 
>/usr/lib/lpd:  lp2: internet domain bind - can't assign requested address
>
>then it hangs... If I type a return, the screen rolls up one line and if
>I type another return, I get:
>
>bba:  ISDN_DMA_ERROR
>
>I can type on the return key till I am blue and it will roll up two
>carriage returns and then give the error.
>
>Is this a correctable error?  What can I do? I do not have distributions
>on this software - is there a way to get the system up in a mini-unix
>mode of some type to correct this problem and then reboot the full kernel
>- I am very depressed because it is my ignorance that has caused this
>problem...
>

Normally, you keep the "generic" kernel /genvmunix around, you should always
be about to boot specifying genvmunix as the kernel file unless there is
something seriously wrong with the hardware.

If all else fails, you can boot the installation media, mount the root disk,
and use that to find and/or fix up things.

>Any of you decstation 5000 experts care to help me out a little?
>

Never had a 5000, so I can't tell you the boot commands.  Try ? at the boot
prompt.  Also try printenv to see what the default boot path is.  Look at the
system administration volumes, and the installation guides for most of the
details.

You may have to boot into single-user mode and edit the /etc/rc stuff to
temporarily take out the network startup until you get it fixed.  On some
UNIX, taking out the network will prevent X windows from starting, I don't
recall if ULTRIX is one of those.

