>a third-party 16 meg memory board, made by Clearpoint?
>
>I have vague recollections that there was some "magic" in getting this
board
>to work, but can't remember details.........failing memory? :-(
>
>Any pointers gratefully accepted.
>
>Current symptoms, when I install the board, I get the usual string of
>self-test messages upto the ...B  where I think it checks memory, it 
>pauses there for a while, then restarts from the beginning of the
power-up
>self test routine. 
>
>	regards, and TIA
>		CrazyCam	Nostalgic and Obsolete Products SIG
>				DECUS Australia


Ah, that clearpoint board.  


As part of the power-up self test, the VAX2000 (actually the VAXstation
2000 without graphics, but who's checking) looks somewhere (and I can't
remember where) about the memory size.  Since DEC supported only a
maximum of 14MB, Clearpoint had to perform some magic to the boot roms
to make the whole thing work.  What they did is made this little tiny
board that "stole" certain ROM addresses from the boot rom and answered
with their data to set the memory correctly.  

How they did this was with a small board that plugged into the ROM
socket and the old ROM plugged into their board. 

Without this, the memory won't work since the system can't figure out
how much RAM is out there, and then does a hard reset.

You should go back to the machine from where you got the 16MB board and
get the ROM module from it.  That'll make things work.

BTW, there was an issue with the ROM module since DEC made a few
versions of htat ROM, and the addresses that the Clearpoint board
"steals" changed a few times.  Your best bet is to either make sure you
and the original machine have the same ROM revision or to swap ROMs
from your machine to the one where the board came from.

If you can't find the ROM module, I suspect you can probably buy one
from Clearpoint, but that was a long time ago and they may not have any
more.


Bill Jackson
Formerly of DEC Workstation Marketing (and someone who had a 16MB board
in  his VAXstation 2000)





