    ues5:~/tmp 204> f90 -O h.f
    ues5:~/tmp 205> time ./a.out 
       400.0000    
    7.602u 0.216s 0:08.37 93.3% 0+20k 0+0io 52pf+0w
    ues5:~/tmp 206> f2c h.f
    ues5:~/tmp 207> gcc -O h.c -lf2c -lm
    ues5:~/tmp 208> time ./a.out
      400.
    42.314u 0.514s 0:44.63 95.9% 0+20k 2+0io 22pf+0w

The program multiplies two large arrays using the "classic" algorithm.

If you needed a good reason to buy DEC Fortran, now you have one.
The other is that f2c is rather unforgiving with non-standard F77 code.
If you don't have to deal with legacy code and you need to compile only
your own code, this might be irrelevant.

