In article <3pal4e$lv@gap.cco.caltech.edu> mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu writes:
>Come on Digital, there is plenty of space on the LK411 keyboard for another
>key - dedicated to <esc>.  It could go left of the existing key, or even
>under the shift key, next to the compose key.  (I'd prefer the former.) 

You might want to take a look at the LK421-AA keyboard.  It's designed
for UNIX users (big CTRL key, no caps lock key, and two different
places for '~' and '`').  It's a lot smaller than a standard Digital
keyboard, because it has no keypad and fewer function keys.  (However,
all of those extra key values are available with a special "extend"
key.)  If you don't use the keypad a lot on VMS, this might work for you.

I believe that the LK421-AA lists for slightly more than the LK411, and
I don't know if it is compatible with the LK411.  It's definitely
a replacement for the LK401.  And it's been available for several years.

-Jeff



