newsgroups: _swat.announce.cc,_trico.computing.sysadmins,comp.unix.pc-clone.32bit,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel,comp.benchmarks
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (Windows; I; 32bit)

>I have not tracked down the list of performance improvments
>necessary for the Pentium.  Is this available on the net
>somewhere?  I did receive information on the first,
>basic enhancement - align all data on natural boundaries
>and/or 8-byte boundaries [just like most RISCs].  This also
>improves 486 timing, but to a lesser extent.  It also uses
>a little more space, naturally.

>The biggest problem area seems to be the stack pointer,
>which is much more efficient when accessed in 8-byte
>chunks.  This apparently is a big change from the 486,
>and is somewhat more troublesome to deal with.

From what I know, the alignment can cause trouble if data is aligned
poorly.  For instance, if you try to access a 32-bit word, you need
to access it on an even 4-byte boundary.  Otherwise, the CPU has to 
retrieve 2 4-byte chunks and combine a part from each.  This would
degrade performance.  A 64-bit word (qword), would then need to be
aligned on even 8-byte boundaries for it's optimal performance.  If
not, it would have to access 4 4-byte chunks and combine a part from
each.  This would decrease performance even greater.  So yes, you
are exactly right.  A Pentium, which uses 64 bit words, needs to 
align its data on EVEN 8-byte boundaries.  In actuallity, RISC 
assembly needs to be done the same way or the same problem will
occur.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

