When we selected our first Alpha, we looked at 4 choices: IBM, who was happy
to loan us a machine but refused to help us get it on our network unless we
paid for the privilege; Sun, who was helpful enough, but was crippled by the
bletcherous Solaris 2.3; HP, who wouldn't even return our phone calls until
I called a friend in management in Cupertino, and then promised us a loaner
machine before going back to not returning phone calls; and DEC, who brought
in a machine when we asked for it, provided what little help we needed to
get it running - which turned out to be damned little, since I could treat
it just like a SysV system and things just worked - and were prompt and
responsive whenever we asked. Which would YOU have chosen? We've been happy
with DEC ever since.

Of course, we come from a production DP environment, where things like
service and support are paramount...your requirements may vary.
