In article <3pal4e$lv@gap.cco.caltech.edu>, mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
writes:

:Any chance that we can convince Digital that putting <esc> and <~> as 
:alternates on the same key is a royal pain in the ass?

   Keyboards are like text editors -- ridiculing any particular one is
   a good way to start a flame war.  (If computer engineers ever rewrote
   the US Constitution and Bill of Rights, you'd likely see these two
   items added to the list of basic `freedoms'. :-)

:Even though I use my LK411 on a VMS system, I do have to connect to Unix
:systems (and POSIX!!!!) about, oh, once an hour.  The DECwindows solution
:of binding that one key to either <~> or <esc> is worthless, I need both as
:keys when working on Unix systems.  Some of us may know that Control-3 is
:<esc>, and so use that, leaving the key permanently set to <~>, but that
:isn't something general users should have to know. 

  First, realize that the traditional UNIX (and POSIX) use of the <ESC>
  key has been known to make some standards folks upset -- it's not a
  legitimate usage under ANSI escape sequence processing.  (I'm not
  intending to imply that either usage is the One True Usage -- just
  highlighting some of the controversy surrounding the the escape key.
  One also sees a similar controversy around the usage of <XON> and
  <XOFF>, these keys are overloaded -- in the form of <CTRL/Q> and
  <CTRL/S> -- by emacs, among other applications.)

:Come on Digital, there is plenty of space on the LK411 keyboard for another
:key - dedicated to <esc>.  It could go left of the existing key, or even
:under the shift key, next to the compose key.  (I'd prefer the former.) 

  Seriously: how many of these (customized) keyboards would folks like
  to buy?  (I don't intend this to sound rude, either -- if there is
  really a market for this change, Digital might be convinced to make
  the changes.)

  Getting back to the topic at hand: one can optionally use <F11> as
  <ESC> on the existing LK401/LK411 keyboards.  (See the DECwindows
  Motif DECterm Options Keyboard <F11> toggle button.)

  Depending on what keyboard plug (mmj & DIN being the two most common)
  the local system uses, one can also use the LK421 (mmj) keyboard:

      +---+---+---+---+---+   +---+---+---+---+---+   +---+---+---+---+
      |F1 |F2 |F3 |F4 |F5 |   |F6 |F7 |F8 |F9 |F10|   | < | v | ^ | > |
      |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |
      +-----+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---------+
      | ESC |!  |@  |#  |$  |%  |^  |&  |*  |(  |)  |_  |+  |   <X]   |
      |     |1  |2  |3  |4  |5  |6  |7  |8  |9  |0  |-  |=  |         |
      +-------+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+
      |  Tab  |Q  |W  |E  |R  |T  |Y  |U  |I  |O  |P  |{  |}  ||  |~  |
      |       |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |[  |]  |\  |`  |
     +---------+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---------+-
     |  Ctrl   |A  |S  |D  |F  |G  |H  |J  |K  |L  |:  |"  |  Return |
     |         |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |;  |'  |         |
     +-----------+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+-----------+
     |  Shift    |Z  |X  |C  |V  |B  |N  |M  |<  |>  |?  |   Shift   |
     |           |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |,  |.  |/  |           |
     -+---+---+-------+---------------------------+-------+-------+---
      |Ext|Com|Alt   #|       <space bar>         |Alt   #|Compose|
      |end|pse|Functn |                           |Functn |       |
      -------------------------------------------------------------

   The <Extend> key allows access to most (all?) of the other functions
   one normally expects to find on an LK401-series keyboard.

