>ONLY required result is a working system.

Sorry, but I've got to disagree... whenever possible, boards, like
anything else, should be repaired.

>If, on the other hand, your requirement is a collectors item with
>all the original parts (or restored to like-new condition, as a car
>might be), then obviously component-level swapping is appropriate.

What difference does something being a collectors item have to do with
anything?  For example, the cars that I drive are over 20 years old,
are not collectors items, and I believe in repairing what goes wrong,
not replacing the entire engine just because something like a camshaft
or a main bearing goes bad.  It's amazing how many so-called
"professional" mechanics don't know how to repair an engine, even a
simple V8 without lots of electronic parts and emissions control
equipment littering the engine compartment.  

It's amazing the number of times that some random mechanic has
reommended that an engine be replaced because only one part needed to
be repaired.  How does one know that the parts in a "remanufactured"
engine are all ground according to proper specs that that the rebuilt
one will last as long as one which is properly repaired?  One doesn't.
Years ago, it was fairly common to be able to take a car to a service
station and have the piston rings replaced... try getting that done
now at these places where changing spark plugs and changing the oil is
regarded as major engine work.

About 10 years ago, we had to have an entire oil-burner assembly
installed simply because a centrifugal switch in the motor of the old
one went bad.  Yep, that's right, the entire assembly (pump, blower,
nozzle, etc.), not just the motor got replaced.  I spent hours on the
telephone trying to find a replacement switch and when that failed, it
was even impossible to find a replacement motor.  That old motor had
lasted well over 30 years, and if repaired, would probably last at
least another 30 years or more.  Unfortunately, it was a cloudy,
bitter-cold day, and the temperature in the house was rapidly
dropping, so, the people who service the furnace were called in and
they replaced the burner assembly.  I still have the old oil-burner
assembly and plan on fixing it to use to replace the new one with.
Now then, if more replacement parts were available, as they should be,
the switch on that motor could easily have been replaced and nothing
else would have been needed.  What a wasteful throw-away society we
live in!

What's really annoying is that many of the people who are responsible
for this being a throw-away society claim to be environmentalists and
buy a new car every other year, replace their home's windows every few
years, buy new major appliances like stoves and refridgerators, etc.
every few years, etc.  What a waste.  Some of these people even
believe that houses are supposed to only last for about 30 to 50
years!

