In article <3pdkp9$da4@darkstar.UCSC.EDU>, arul@cse.ucsc.edu (Arul
Ananthanarayanan) writes:
>
>I've had numerous complaints from users about the bell on
>our Alphastation 200 4/166 machines. Specifically
>they are upset about the delay in hearing
>the beep, or sometimes not hearing the beep at all.
>
>Is there anything that can be done about this? 
>
>Can we expect the same behavior on the Alphastation
>200 4/100 and 250 4/266 machines we have on order?
>

  The systems that come with a pre-installed sound card can have
  distorted and inaudable bell tone output.  The sound card snoops
  the square wave generator and simulates the output, so that
  normal beeps can be heard.  It doesn't work too well.

  A workaround is to follow the speaker wire from the speaker to
  the sound card, and disconnect it from the sound card.  Directly
  next to the sound card on the motherboard is a 4-pin connector
  labeled SPEAKER - plug the speaker wire into it (doesn't matter
  which way).

  You will now get pure tones for the bell... but the sound card
  will not be able to generate output through the built-in speaker.
  Instead you will need to connect external speakers.  Of course,
  if you are doing any serious multimedia, you will already have
  external speakers for the sound card.

