In article <1995May9.134308.26173@lugb.latrobe.edu.au>, CCPN@luff.latrobe.edu.au (Paul Nankervis) writes:
> Aside from various development tools and Win32 APIs for VMS just what
> is being offered here? 

> Will Microsoft provide tools like
> Word and Excel for VMS? 

The only native Microsoft tools (beyond the Win32 API) being offered VMS users
will be a VMS Visual C++ compiler.  Whatever Visual C++ source code you write
for WNT, using Win32 API's, will also compile using on VMS using Digital's VMS
Visual C++ compiler. 

> Are any changes for NT planned? 

They plan on giving WNT "VMS Client New File System" services.  With this you
can run VMS's New File System on your WNT system and your files will be stored
on the VMS system.  With the new file system being a log file system you will
get a very robust file system with real online backup.  Since log file systems
just keep adding entries in log file each time you make a file change, as
opposed today's system that replaces the on disk structure, online backups are
very reliable - you just copy the log from time point X to time point Y. 

> And has DEC sold its soul to MicroSoft?

I would phrase the question as has DEC sold the soul of its VMS base to
Microsoft? 

It depends on whether you believe Digital has provided enough of the Microsoft
development environment that folks will remain happy on VMS.  If they only
provided enough to wet the appetite the problem will be if the users like what
they see, but are still hungry, they may decide to satisfy their hunger with
real WNT rather than WNT-lite (VMS).  In the end it will boil down to whether 
the users see VMS as WNT-lite or WNT as VMS-lite.

The other issue is to what extent Digital's strategy offers something when 3
tier client server computing exceeds the need and 2 tier does well enough that
the additional expense of 3 tier is not justifiable.  If Digital's answer is
to write off 2 tier for VMS they will place themselves in the same trap they
did in the late 1980's with VMS.  The trap is the following... 

	1) If your strategy is to leverage pioneer technology leadership
	as your unique value in the marketplace, while writing off competing
	in the commodity segments, you build in high cost because of R&D.

	2) The commodity segments of the market will also mature.  They
	often do so by cherry picking your R&D efforts.  In fact they get
	to implement without initial legacy API customer support issues.

	3) At some point in time the commodity market produces something
	that is good enough to solve the problem that the justification
	for the added expense of using the pioneer technology provider goes
	away.

	4) The trap then springs - the company who's primary marketplace
	value is providing technology leadership can't compete on price 
	because its cost structure is too high and looses the market.

One can easy argue that the Japanese and Microsoft have been very adept at
playing this gaming.  Let someone else do the bleeding edge R&D and then when
the technology model matures come in with a well done, but not better,
extremely cost effective implementation...

--
Jeff Killeen | EMAIL:killeen@decus.org | FAX:508.478.9889



