In article <3p36kh$716@agate.berkeley.edu>, peterm@tonks.EECS.Berkeley.EDU
(Peter Mardahl) wrote:

>Furthermore, lmf never listed "OSF-USR-IMPLICIT-BASE" as one of the installed
>PAK's.

That's because it isn't a real PAK, thus the name "IMPLICIT".  The base
PAK throws this on there when there isn't another USR PAK around when it
gets installed, at least from what I can tell.  It certainly isn't a
logical way of doing things - you'd think that the implicit PAK would
automatically quiet down when another USR PAK, with a higher limit, comes
along.  DEC has a lot of kinks to work out of this system, IMO.

I've had this problem often.  I guess the best way to go is to delete the
implicit PAK, and then install the multi-user PAK.  It also seems there is
an unload/load procedure you need to do.  Needlessly complex, I think.

