In article <bmike-1005951652360001@dieckmann.cc.oberlin.edu>,
Michael Dieckmann <bmike@ocvaxc.cc.oberlin.edu> wrote:
>Is there any good formula, chart, or other reference for comparing VAXen
>to Alphas?  For example, comparing a VAX 6420 to the current Alpha line? 
>I'm thinking particularly in terms of CPU performance running Open VMS.

A VAX 6410 is rated at 7.0 VUPS (according to a pocket reference I
have). A 6420 should be a 2-cpu version of this.

A VAX 11/780 is rated at 1.0 VUPS, and 1.0 SpecInt92 (and 1.0
SpecFp92). VUPS/SpecInt is *NOT* related to each other, but on most
older VAX'en you can get a reasonable estimate of the SpecInt by using
the VUPS rating of one of the processor (if more than one).

Then you have to consider the fact that the machine had more than one
CPU. Depending on the workload this may or may not give more
performance (up to almost twice in this case).

It's harder to estimate SpecFp, and I haven't an idea how to quantify
that. It varies much more between different VAX cpus.

Anyway, it shouldn't be any problem getting SpecInt figures for a new
machine. 

It won't give you more than an indication, but if you need more than
an indication you probably should try to run some programs on an Alpha
instead.



