In article <3otiij$d9n@cpca6.uea.ac.uk>,
J.Brainard <zebra@cpca6.uea.ac.uk> wrote:
>%X Toolkit Warning: locale not supported by C library, locale unchanged
>
>Meaning?

It means that the program tried to switch to the locale someone/you
had specified, but that the locale didn't exist.

Setting LANG to bogus values seems to trigger this warning (I thought
that LOCALE might do it too, but that doesn't seem to be the case).

My guess is that someone has set LANG, but failed to use to correct
name on the locale or mis-spelled. Common reasons for setting this is
to change the date/time format or change the behavior of some programs
(such as vi) with regard to special national characters (such as the
swedish ������).

The last item (special characters) can be handled using the more
specific LC_CTYPE instead (which is what we use, as we do want vi to
be able to handle special characters, but we're not interrested in
swedish dates from 'ls -l'!)



