>>In article <3p55d9$1rs@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>,
>>A.R. Duell <ard12@eng.cam.ac.uk> wrote:
> 
>>>Am I the only person who believes that these old machines should be 
>>>repaired to component level. I assume that the Heathkit manuals included 
> 
>>No, you're not the only one.  I also posted a message about this.  I
>>was shocked that someone would would want to perform "board swapping"
>>for a Heathkit, particularly when one has all of the manuals (which
> 
>I'm shocked anyone would want to perform 'board swapping' at all, 
>particularly on an older machine...
> 

Let me tell you why I, and everyone else I know who toils to keep
vital PDP-11's still doing useful work,  do 'board swapping':

There are still tens of thousands (very likely, hundreds of thousands)
of PDP-11's out there in the real-time world.  What does "real-time"
mean?  The best definition I've seen is that a system that is "real-time"
depends on a computer in such a way that if an answer from the computer
is late, by even microseconds, then the system is broken.  Worthless.
Hundreds of thousands, or millions, of dollars of equipment that the
computer interacts with isn't available.  Vital scientific experiments
go bad, factories stop producing, etc.

In applications such as these, where the PDP-11 is still king of the hill,
not only are there always spare boards to get the systems up and going
as fast as possible, but there are often entire spare systems that get
swapped in at the first sign of trouble.  I've seen systems with thousands
of lines of digital and analog I/O that can be switched over to a fallback
system with the push of a single button.  As soon as the fallback system
is switched in, the original system is put under diagnostics
and the bad board (or system unit, or BA11) is pulled and a spare put
in, because this system may now have to serve as a fallback system anytime
now.

Anyone who makes the suggestion that component-level repairs should always
be made to such a computer, instead of board swaps, doesn't understand
the economic realities of these real-time systems.  Especially today, when
Unibus-era hardware is available for scrap-metal (i.e. "by-the-ton")
prices, it makes absolutely no sense not to have spare boards, system
units, etc., available for quick repairs.

Of course, if it makes sense to do so, the bad board will be repaired.
(Or the heads realigned, or the backplane cleaned, etc.)
But very rarely does it make any sense to do so.  Why would you pay a
technician even half an hour to do this when you can buy a working spare
for $10 or $20 in most cases?

Needless to say, as a hobbyist, my standards are a bit different.  I'm much
more likely to repair a personal system to a much lower level than a
system at work.  (And many of my personal systems were built from boards,
boxes, etc. that were not candidates for commercial repair so I ended
up with them for free!)  But this is only because I have the luxury of time,
and lack dozens of people demanding that the system has to back up
and running *now*.

Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)
Kellogg Radiation Lab, Caltech.



