In article <3orcfu$noc@news.acns.nwu.edu>, tilden@nuhepl.phys.nwu.edu (Robert Tilden) writes:
=Hi,
=	I posted an article several days ago concerning a semi-crashed
=hard drive I'm trying to extract data from. 
=
=	I can mount the drive /foreign and use DUMP to examine data on 
=the drive. I've used DUMP/FILE to dump the indexf.sys areas and I now
=have a formatted list of file names, physical locations, and sizes. Does
=anyone have an idea on how to read a specific range of blocks off a 
=/foreign mounted drive? Better yet, a piece of code which will do it?
=
=	I know you're out there, I can hear you breathing...

Sure.  You just open the disk as a file, and use direct access I/O to read the
blocks you're interested in.  Note that on the disk mounted /FOREIGN, you're
dealing with logical blocks, so the block numbering is zero-based.  E.g., in
FORTRAN, to read the sixth block on disk PDA0:, you'd:

	BYTE BUFFER(512)
	OPEN(UNIT=1,FILE='PDA0:',FORM='UNFORMATTED',ACCESS='DIRECT',
	1	STATUS='OLD')
	READ(1'5) BUFFER
	END
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
