Message-ID: <3os6vf$o3r@agate.berkeley.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 102448318
references: <RLM.95Apr19090451@netcom14.netcom.com> <RLM.95May10092232@netcom5.netcom.com> <1995May10.203923.8518@malad2.mala.bc.ca>
organization: Plasma Theory and Simulation Group
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <1995May10.203923.8518@malad2.mala.bc.ca>,
Malcolm Dunnett <dunnett@mala.bc.ca> wrote:
>In article <RLM.95May10092232@netcom5.netcom.com>, 
>
>speak to your situation, but I've installed dozens of PAKS and *never*
>found one that didn't work right the first time (except when I entered
>the information wrong).

Actually, I have had mild problems getting PAK's working.  Maybe it was
just my stupidity, but I had trouble getting the multi-user PAK to operate
along with the "base" PAK for DEC osf/1.  I simple-mindedly just added
the multi-user PAK, and activated it, but it didn't work even after a
reboot.  I had to remove the "base" PAK, put the multi-user PAK on
FIRST, then put the base PAK on, to get the multi-user PAK recognized by
OSF.  Annoying, but it DID work.

However, I consider this much superior to any dongle.  You shouldn't
have to mess with pieces of your hardware to get your software working.
Imagine the nightmare if you had many dongle'd software packages,
as the previous poster described.

I don't understand how PAK's provide any security against piracy
however.  What prevents you from simply stealing a PAK?  Is it keyed
off of a serial number in your machine?  That seems impossible since
