In article <3os6vf$o3r@agate.berkeley.edu>, 
   peterm@alfven.eecs.berkeley.edu (Peter Mardahl) writes:
> 
> Actually, I have had mild problems getting PAK's working.  Maybe it was
> just my stupidity, but I had trouble getting the multi-user PAK to operate
> along with the "base" PAK for DEC osf/1.  I simple-mindedly just added
> the multi-user PAK, and activated it, but it didn't work even after a
> reboot.  I had to remove the "base" PAK, put the multi-user PAK on
> FIRST, then put the base PAK on, to get the multi-user PAK recognized by
> OSF.  Annoying, but it DID work.
> 

    I'm getting the impression that the LMF implementation in OSF/1 may
not be as solid as that in VMS ( where I've done all my PAK work ). If
true this represents a bug in OSF/1 which needs addressing, but certainly
doesn't argue against the PAK concept in general.

> 
> I don't understand how PAK's provide any security against piracy
> however.  What prevents you from simply stealing a PAK?  Is it keyed
> off of a serial number in your machine?  That seems impossible since
> the multi-user PAK I have was campus-wide and is used on many, many
> machines.
> 

    It doesn't.

    But if you get caught with a PAK which doesn't belong to you on
your machine it's going to be pretty hard to argue "gee, how'd that
get there". By using the PAK system Digital can freely distribute all
their software on CD, with the knowledge that it won't work unless
someone is in posession of a PAK ( or hacks the O/S - which BTW is
also a violation of your license agreement with Digital, under the
"reverse engineering" clause ). So the PAK is not intended to stop
dishonest people from cheating, but rather to prove they were 
knowingly cheating.

    The PAK also allows software to have an expiry date ( for demo
purposes or periodic licensing like CSLG ) and to be tied to 
particular release dates of the software ( to enforce the "right to
new version" clause by ensuring new versions of software won't run
with an outdated PAK but allowing the old version to run forever )


