In article <3o9gr8$cct@raba.raba.com>, bill.rye@raba.com writes:
> Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com (Peter Mayne) wrote:
>> 
>> At the moment, Digital ships you a CD every few months with a whole
>> bunch of software on it. 
> I haven't received any.  I think you have to "pay" for this luxury.

That is the nature of life, someone must pay for the efforts of others.
Those who do not require regular updates can buy the CD-ROMs at odd
intervals.  Those who never want to upgrade (there are some), need
never pay.  That is better than making everyone pay for the highest
level service.

>> How do you propose that Digital wakes up? What better scheme would
>> you propose that keeps the convenience? A simple "get rid of PAKs"
>> is not sufficient, because then the CD distribution method must
>> also disappear. 
> Perhaps drop the "VMS" based PAK approach and look at how other
> UN*X vendors have done their licenses.  PAKS are great if you're
> background is VMS, if you're background is UN*X, they're alien.

Prior to V5.0, VMS did not have PAKs.  Thousands of VMS administrators
managed to figure out how to type @SYS$UPDATE:VMSLICENSE.  I was one of
them.  I prefer VMS to Unix, but I don't think the Unix administrators
of the world are dumber than the VMS administrators of the world (in
fact, my bias is that Unix administration is more challenging), so
what makes PAKs more alien to Unix administrators than they were to VMS
administrators when VMS V5.0 was released ?

>> Software *is* easy to pirate: if Digital sent
>> everybody a CD every few months with no PAKs, how many unlicensed
>> copies do you think there'd be?
> And hacking the kernel can defeat PAKS.  I don't think the number
> of unlicensed copies would equate to the number of machines that can 
> mount the CD. I don't have a use for 99% of the software on the CD's 
> or the disk space to install all of it.

And likewise, pirates do not have infinite disk space, so they would
be unlikely to steal software they did not intend to use.

At least in the US, however, businesses are at considerable risk for
court damages if they are found to be pirating software.  In the PC
world the reckoning comes in the form of "raids" on individual companies
with auditors checking the software on each PC and potentially finding
management guilty of tolerating their people stealing software.  An
atmosphere of casual disinterest where software happens to get copied
illegally is something for which courts have held management liable.
In many cases customer management looks to vendors to provide controls
within software to guarantee software is used in accordance with the
payments management has been made.

Larry Kilgallen
LJK Software



