In article <3oo5db$7m8@dwarf.mh.blyth.com>, Steve Rooke <Steve_Rooke@mh.blyth.com> writes:
|Firstly, please excuse my ignorance of all matters VAX/DEC.
|
|I have two sites connected by IP, both have clients but only one site has
|some servers that need to be accessed via DECnet.  I really need to access
|these servers from the other site and guess this can be done via some
|form of
|tunneling.  I know there is a product called TCPware but this would need
|to be installed on 'servers' at each end and I don't have this situation.
|What I'm really looking for is either a small black-box or some software
|to run under U*ix.  For our very limited traffic, the cheaper the
|better.
|
|Any suggestions please?

Assuming the constraint that your "DECnet servers" do not already
have something to allow you to run DECnet apps over IP - such as 
our Phase/IP or DEC's DECnet/OSI/IP, then you would need to buy
a pair of boxes, one at each end of the IP link, and use them 
to tunnel DECnet thru IP.

I believe that those boxes would have to be VAX/VMS systems and
would need to have some TCP/IP package that supports tunnelling
DECnet thru IP - our MultiNet or (I infer from your post) TCPware.
Note that this needn't be fantasically expensive - all you would
need at each end is a tubeless VS2000 with 6MB and an RD54 - should
be about $400 or less.  Of course, you'd need the IP package software
for it, and someone with a bit of VMS to install and configure
the software and keep an eye on it.

Cheers,

