You seem to be atypical.

>Haven't they heard of dongles?  They're much more secure, and a lot
>less painful.  As far as I've seen, a dongle is much less of a pain in
>the ass than a PAK that can get wiped with today's disk crash, or
>cosmic ray strike, or whatever.

How do you hang a dongle on a 370 channel? That's why software keys are the
coming thing in the mainframe world. Dongles are generally evil things,
because they use up hardware resources that could go to better use. We'd
have a string of dongles longer than our machine room on the back of our
2100 if we had dongles for every piece of software we have licensed.

My problem with PAKs as DEC implemented them is with their lack of
dedicated, 24x365 support. Other than that, they're quite easy to deal with.
We keep the PAKs associated with our machine next to the machine, and they
go with it wherever it goes. I can't help it if your customer made your life
miserable...but that's a problem that a dongle wouldn't alleviate - they
could have shipped the wrong dongle just as easily as the wrong PAK. As for
a disk crash, that's what backups are for...you DO do those, right?

Software licensing splutions beat hardware ones every time. If you're going
to recommend against DEC for using them, then that's your customers' loss.
