From: dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett)
Subject: Death to Dongles ( was Re: DEC "openness")
Date: 1995/05/10
Message-ID: <1995May10.203923.8518@malad2.mala.bc.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 102448257
references: <RLM.95Apr19090451@netcom14.netcom.com>  <RLM.95May10092232@netcom5.netcom.com>
organization: Malaspina University-College
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <RLM.95May10092232@netcom5.netcom.com>, 
    rlm@netcom.com (Robert McMillin) writes:
> 
> Haven't they heard of dongles?  They're much more secure, and a lot
> less painful.  As far as I've seen, a dongle is much less of a pain in
> the ass than a PAK that can get wiped with today's disk crash, or
> cosmic ray strike, or whatever.

   You're joking, right?

   Dongles are one of the most obnoxious security schemes ever invented.

   I'm not sure how a PAK, being a piece of paper, is going to get
"wiped with today's disk crash". When you restore your backup the
database entry for the PAK will be good as new, and even without a backup
the worst case scenario is that you have to re-enter the data. I can't
speak to your situation, but I've installed dozens of PAKS and *never*
found one that didn't work right the first time (except when I entered
the information wrong).

   A dongle, on the other hand, contains active electronic components.
Should one of these fail the dongle is worthless and there's no way,
short of being couriered a replacement, that you can possibly get your
software to work. Dongles also require a particular hardware interface
( generally a Centronics compatible parallel port ) which they can plug
into. What if the system I want to run the software on isn't equipped
with one, or it's not working for some reason. I have seen cases where
a dongle refused to work in an otherwise working parallel port, or
caused the printer attached to stop working when the dongle was
installed. 

   Also, how do you scale the "dongle" concept to cover dozens ( or even
hundreds ) of different products. The mind boggles to consider a stack
of dongles several yards long sticking out the back of ones system.

   I'll take you at your word that you had a bad experience with a PAK, but
in my experience this is not a typical situation and generally speaking
PAKs are light years ahead of dongles in terms of ease of software
management.

-- 
