Remember that the internals of Win NT are modeled after VMS.  One of Win NT's
problems (imho) is the lack of X Windows as a supported API.  This prevents
TRUELY portable modern codes (there's more money to be made in Windows than all
X systems combined).  Thus, the WIN32s API will let VMS support modern PC-style
codes... the compilers are there, now so will the GUI.

When DEC finishes the new file system, it is a natural for Microsoft to include
it in NT for lots of reasons.  DCL is just an interpretor (and loaded at that-
rember the {virtually never used} CLI field in Authorize?).  SO... we now have
the languages, the APIs (GUI), the file system, and user-choosable script
language.   Hmmm... The conclusion isn't that far a leap that VMS may not be so
dead after all.

