In article <3oof1s$bn7@newshost.lanl.gov>, John Benner  <benner> wrote:
>Is there a way to automatically set the DISPLAY variable when logging
>into a remote Alpha running OSF? I log into an Alpha frequently from
>a variety of other machines (SGI's) and want to avoid manually
>setting the display to the machine I am logging in from. Any help would
>be appreciated.
>
Here is what I use in my .profile file.  It work for ALPHA OSF1, ULTRIX,
and some versions of SUN OS.

#
# set DISPLAY
#
if [ "$0" = "-ksh" ]
then
   #
   # FIXME: if you rsh from workstation A to server B, the DISPLAY is set
   # correctly, however, if you then rsh from server B to server B (or to
   # another workstation), then the display is set to server B, 
   #
   HOST=${DISPLAY%%:*}
   if [ -z "$HOST" ]
   then
      HOST=$(who am i | awk -F'(' '{ print $2 }')
      HOST=${HOST%%\)*}
      if [ -z "$HOST" ]
      then
         HOST=$(who -Mm | awk -F'(' '{ print $2 }')
	 HOST=${HOST%%\)*}
      fi 
      if [ -z "${DISPLAY}" ]
      then
         DISPLAY="${HOST}:0.0"
      fi
   fi
   export DISPLAY
fi
-- 
