In article <TOMK.95May12170103@kobra.csd.uu.se>, tomk@csd.uu.se (Tom Karlsson) writes:
:
:Today I got in touch with my first AlphaStation to administrate and
:configure - didn't turn out so well...
:
:After carefully writing down the SYSTEM's password (OpenVMS) on a paper
:and then enter it when configuring the system (first boot), I also typed
:it again to verify and everything was OK so far.
:Later, after all the PAKs was inserted, the machine rebooted - *but* when
:I typed the SYSTEM username and my password at the login window, I got an
:'authorization failure'. Something must have gone wrong :-/
:
:I called DEC about it and they said they would call me back, but this is
:friday afternoon and no response yet.
:Is there any way I can change the password from the boot prompt, so that
:I can log into the machine ?
:
:I have no manuals except for the 'User Information' and no prior experience
:of Alphas and OpenVMS (I'm a regular UN*X-guy :-)
:
:Please, if you have an answer, send me an E-mail or post it here.

  Halt the system -- typically via a <halt> button somewhere on the system
  enclosure or by pressing <BREAK> (often the <F5> key) on the console
  terminal.

  Since this is an OpenVMS Alpha system, request a conversational bootstrap
  using the console terminal command: 

  >>> b -fl x,1 ddcu

  You will have to insert the system root value (x) and the boot device
  (ddcu) for your local system.  If you are not sure about thus, use the
  console help to learn about the commands used to display the environment
  variables.  (You're interested in the boot flags and the boot device.)

  Once the bootstrap has started, you should (eventually) see the following
  prompt, and should enter the following commands:

  SYSBOOT> SET UAFALTERNATE 1
  SYSBOOT> SET WRITESYSPARAMS 0
  SYSBOOT> CONTINUE

  You will now be able to log in (assuming no one has set up an alternate
  system authorization file on the system) from the console terminal (and
  _only_ from the console terminal), and you can use SYSTEM and any old
  passwords you like.

  Once you have logged in, "SET DEFAULT SYS$SYSTEM", SET PROCESS/PRIV=ALL,
  issue a DEASSIGN for any SYSUAF* logical names ("SHOW LOGICAL SYSUAF*",
  then "DEASSIGN/SYSTEM/EXEC SYSUAF" them -- look specifically for the
  logical name that references SYSUAFALT), and RUN AUTHORIZE.  You will
  (should) now be able to reset the password:

  UAF> MODIFY SYSTEM/PASSWORD=ObscureAndMemorable

  And you'll be back in business.  There is no need to reboot.

  The above is a variation on the procedure documented in the OpenVMS manual
  set.  You _will_ want to order manuals for the system.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
