>swapped in at the first sign of trouble.  I've seen systems with thousands

Sure. And then you repair the one you've swapped out. Been there, done that.

>Of course, if it makes sense to do so, the bad board will be repaired.
>(Or the heads realigned, or the backplane cleaned, etc.)
>But very rarely does it make any sense to do so.  Why would you pay a
>technician even half an hour to do this when you can buy a working spare
>for $10 or $20 in most cases?

Because (a) you can't always find a spare, (b) a lot of them cost a lot more,
(c) 2nd hand cards need not work correctly (I don't always trust 
diagnostics, and if I have to test a possibly dubious card to component 
level, I could use the time to repair a card that I know the faults of), 
and (d) it's (often) a lot quicker and cheaper to fix the old card than 
to find a replacement.

>Needless to say, as a hobbyist, my standards are a bit different.  I'm much
>more likely to repair a personal system to a much lower level than a
>system at work.  (And many of my personal systems were built from boards,
>boxes, etc. that were not candidates for commercial repair so I ended
>up with them for free!)  But this is only because I have the luxury of time,
>and lack dozens of people demanding that the system has to back up
>and running *now*.

I've had people demanding machines back _now_. I've even once been asked 
when a (non-essential) printer would be back on line, when I had a 'scope 
connected to the controller board and was trying to figure out just why 
it wasn't starting... They were somewhat annoyed that it could take a few 
days, even though there were several other printers available for use.

I've also done some pretty fast repairs to component level (often in less 
time than it would take the service engineer to drive over). I once came 
in one morning to find a monitor dead, but that DEC had been called. I 
fixed it, cased it up _before_ they arrived :-)

Anyway, the original posting was about an H11 in a second-hand shop. I 
suspect it was going to be bought by a hobbyist, and that it would not be 
an essential machine. Therefore, to claim that spares would be hard to 
find _from a hobbyist viewpoint_ was downright incorrect.


>Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)
>Kellogg Radiation Lab, Caltech.

-tony




