In article <15MAY199509435038@vx.cis.umn.edu>, tkennedy@vx.cis.umn.edu (Tim Kennedy) writes:
:>The Alphastation 200/250/400 series uses an IBM PS/2 8 pin mini-DIN connector
:>just as in most modern PC's, NOT the 4-wire connector used in the 3000 series.
:
:I have an "LK250" with such a plug: is this what it goes to? Never did know.

   The LK250 was an LK200-series keyboard (DEC VT 105 key layout) made
   available for PCs and clones, and available on the VAXmate PC.  It
   was largely replaced by the LK450 (DEC VT 108 key layout), which has
   itself been replaced by the LK411/LK412 (also DEC VT 108 key layout).
   (There is a device driver available for PCs for the LK450, not sure
   about the driver availability/necessity for the LK41x series keyboard.)

:Also found that VT320 can remap the ~ key to be (esc)... maybe Alphastations 
:also can do this. (Ain't got one, so jus' a guess)

   The DECwindows Motif DECterm is able to remap keys -- on OpenVMS VAX
   and Alpha.  Select Options Keyboard from the menu, and you can change
   the mapping on <~`>, <F11>, <<>>, and <,<> and <.>>.  One can also
   alter the key and mouse definitions used by Motif -- what happens when
   various mouse buttons are pressed with the sprite (cursor) on the window
   borders, on the background, etc.

