In <1995May15.133810.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu> fairfield@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu writes:

>        My LK401-AA US/UK has a  key  in the lower left-hand corner with
>    keycap  +---+.  The comma and period keys are marked +---+ and +---+.
>            | < |                                        | < |     | > |
>            | > |                                        | , |     | . |
>            +---+                                        +---+     +---+
>    By default, the comma and period keys  send comma and period in both
>    when  shifted and unshifted.  There's a setup option (yes,  OpenVMS,
>    DECterm) to map the first key,  "<>",  to  send  "`~",  and  another
>    option  to  map  the comma key to send ",<" (and by implication, the
>    period key to send ".>").  So  it  seems pretty clear to me, that if
>    you  remap the key in the upper left-hand corner +---+ to send  ESC,
>                                                     | ~ |
>                                                     | ` |
>                                                     +---+
>    then you put the tilde and grave  accent down on "<>", and then pick
>    up  the  angle  brackets on the shifted comma and period  keys.   So
>    what's the problem?

The problem is that no matter what you do, you will end up with a 
mislabeled key.  I don't find this solution particularly elegant.  Do you?

Another problem is that once you are in front of somebody else's keyboard,
your tricks won't work and you'll have to figure out the tricks used by
that person.

My solution was to compromise only one symbol, `, so the `/~ key has been
remapped to ESC/~ and ` was moved to the >/< key (for consistency, I also
duplicated ~ to SHIFT >/<).  This was of little help when I had to use
the system of one of my colleagues, who was very fond of the >/< key and
who needed the F11 key to act as F11, so he relocated ESC to... Help,
which he never used.  Until I figured out this, I had to press CTRL/[ to
get ESC :-(

So, yes, DEC gratuitously botched the LK401 layout.  Even the nice
dxkeycaps cannot fully compensate the damage.

This is probably not a problem for the OpenVMS user, who doesn't need
ESC in his environment (unless he telnets to a Unix system), but it's
definitely a problem for the OSF/1 user who gets a keyboard which was
obviously not designed for his needs.

Dan
