distribution: world
references: <3n6cs8$hqn@amhux3.amherst.edu> <3n7js2$ghg@jac.zko.dec.com> <3od1ej$vit@cpca6.uea.ac.uk>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: giles@fig.nacto.lkg.dec.com (Leslie Giles)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I've been involved in trying to implement a development environment and
simple SCM system on an Athena network, and I've ended up disliking Athena.
I came up with a list of problems which I'm using to justify the move from
Athena to NFS/NIS/Automounter, with the total support of the developers who
need to use the system.

This list may be useful to people out there who are considering Athena.

Lezz Giles

-----------------------------------------------------------
Problems with Athena environment

This note contains two lists - the first lists the standard
Unix facilities and tools that do not work in an Athena
environment and other problems.  The second lists the fundamental
inconsistencies between an Athena environment and an NFS/NIS/AMD
environment that cause these problems.


1) It is significantly more difficult to find system
administrators who know Athena than who know the standard
NFS/NIS/AMD environment.

2) There is a learning curve for new developers coming into
an Athena environment.  Most will be coming from an NFS/NIS/AMD
environment.

3) ~user does not work, e.g. I can't copy a file from Brendan's
home directory with "cp ~hannigan/file ."

4) Filesystems must be "attach"ed before they can be used.  One
advantage of the automounter is that if you want a file, it is there
automatically.

5) su and rlogin don't work.

6) Normal unix mail and related enhancements (e.g. xbiff and the emacs
mail scripts) do not work.

7) rsh doesn't work.

8) setuid scripts don't work.

9) telnet is disabled in the standard configuration and it cannot
be enabled without opening a large security hole.

10) The following Unix functions do not always work:
getpwuid(), getpwnam()

11) The standard environment files, .login, .cshrc, .profile and
.env, do not have their normal use of per-user environment
customization.

12) Permissions disappear transparently if you have used telnet to
log into a system for more than around 8 hours, or if you have
a server process running for more than around 8 hours.

13) ls doesn't know how to map uid/gid to usernames and group names.
This means that it is not trivial to find out who owns a file.

14) It is difficult to use NFS to mount "Kerberized" Unix
filesystems on to DOS or NT workstations, on to other Unix
workstations e.g. Sun, HP, or on to other products, e.g. Gigaswitch/ATM.

15) Third party tools typically assume an NFS/NIS/AMD environment
and do not work optimally in an Athena environment, e.g. Clearcase.

16) Athena typically lags Digital Unix by one version, i.e. it is
not possible to use Athena with the latest version of Digital Unix.

17) It is not possible to ftp to a system using a personal user id
when that user is not already logged into the system.

------------------------------------------------------------
The fundamental problems with the Athena environment, which lead
to these problems, are listed below.  The items above are listed
against the responsible fundamental problem.

Generally the combination of all the problems leads to an
environment which isn't Unix - 1,2,15,16

a) The passwd database on each workstation is not maintained by
Hesiod (the Athena counterpart of NIS) - 3, 5, 10, 13, 17

b) Kerberos tickets are associated with a user id and not with a
login session - 7, 9

c) Kerberos tickets are needed for all nfs file accesses - 8, 14

d) Kerberos tickets expire after a period of time - 12

e) Mail is implemented in a completely different way - 6

f) Filesystems need to be explicitly "mounted" or attached - 4

g) Much of the Athena system is driven by user-visible settings, e.g.
environment variables and Athena-specific versions of standard Unix
tools - 11



