Overnight, my mailbox filled up with messages explaining
that Intel released most or all (small dispute) of the
performance-related info. back in 1994 sometime, and that
Intel even assisted in the recent gcc enhancements, now
being beta-tested (I understand).

I have not tracked down the list of performance improvments
necessary for the Pentium.  Is this available on the net
somewhere?  I did receive information on the first,
basic enhancement - align all data on natural boundaries
and/or 8-byte boundaries [just like most RISCs].  This also
improves 486 timing, but to a lesser extent.  It also uses
a little more space, naturally.

The biggest problem area seems to be the stack pointer,
which is much more efficient when accessed in 8-byte
chunks.  This apparently is a big change from the 486,
and is somewhat more troublesome to deal with.

So far I have not seen anything about optimal instruction 
packing. [e.g. packing address/program control, loads/stores, 
and ALU operations into [[128?]]-bit chunks.]



