>|> with anything useful?  I'm being naive, no doubt, but
>|> this kind of thing has been puzzled out before (I won't
>|> even call it reverse-engineering; more like imitate
>|> success and see what happens...)
>
>Still wondering if anybody out there playing around
>with gcc or whatever [no NDA information please!] has 
>been able to play around with optimization and get a 
>decent Pentium boost over code optimized for a 486.

>If so, what is the magic?
 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

No magic, most likely. There are 2 simple things that a
Pentium-aware compiler can do that will show a significant
improvement over a 386/486 type compiler. The first is to
re-arrange instructions so that pipeline pairing occurs,
according to the Pentium pairing rules. The second thing
is to eliminate AGI delays (address generation interlocks).
The 486 has these too, but only the previous instruction
can cause one. On the Pentium it is possible for an 
instruction to cause an AGI 3 instructions away. 

Even mild application of these techniques can provide a good
performance boost.

Mike Schmit

