In article <3p81n7$2bh@truffula.fp.trw.com>, brad@truffula.fp.trw.com (Brad Brahms) writes:
:
:The Scenario:
:
:Okay, here's what happened: Turn you DEC 3000 (tower) on.  One hour latter,
:<click> it goes off.  Spend time with DEC on the phone getting support
:(they were pretty nice).  DEC determines its a power supply (e.g. per
:symptoms).  DEC comes to replace supply, and low and behold, the Air
:Movement Sensor had detected a fault (not enough air movement) because
:the fan wouldn't turn.  Oh, oh, the DEC person doesn't have a fan.
:Lets see....
:
:The Trick (straight from DEC):
:
:Turn the 3000 on its side to get the fan to move.  Presto, the 3000 is
:back up and running.
:
:Questions:
:
:Would it have been to hard to have the OS/monitor to give a message to
:the user why the machine was going to die, instead of just going down?
:Wouldn't a heat sensor have been better?  Why would it not go back on
:until a DEC engineer figured it out?  It would have been nice to at
:least dies during the self-diag saying that the Air Sensor does not
:detect enough air movement.
:
:Anyway, it works, and no lost data.  Just a few more gray hairs and...
:Life's a beach (replace with noun of choice).

  Heat can build up in various parts of the enclosure if there is little
  (and especially if there is no) airflow -- sensing airflow is rather
  more likely to be reliable than a similar number of thermal sensors,
  particularly when the thermal condition is due to the lack of airflow.
  (One part of the box can get rather hot, and convection might or might
  not move the heat to the location of the sensor.)

  The airflow shutdown mechanism -- the sensor -- is generally hooked
  directly to the power supply, and it effectively "pulls the plug" when
  a problem is detected.  This is a critical `self preservation' fault.
  I have heard of thermal sensors piggy-backed onto the airflow sensors,
  to detect overtemperatures that the fans are unable to compensate for.
  (This can be caused by a high ambient temperature.)

  I don't know what happened in your particular situation -- from your
  description, it sounds like an Air Moving Device (I'm not kidding, that
  is what a fan is sometimes called around here :-) failed.  I have seen
  systems with an air vent blocked by papers, and I have seen systems that
  were sitting with the air vents aimed down into the carpeting -- these
  can trigger the same shutdown sequence.

