Typical, expect new hires to know everything already.  Much better
to hire people who know how to learn.


>2) There is a learning curve for new developers coming into
>an Athena environment.  Most will be coming from an NFS/NIS/AMD
>environment.

What's so difficult about it?  In my experience, most new hires
are coming from a DOS/Windows environment anyway.


>3) ~user does not work, e.g. I can't copy a file from Brendan's
>home directory with "cp ~hannigan/file ."

Works in our environment just fine.  At any rate, what's the big deal?
Afraid to type a few characters?


>4) Filesystems must be "attach"ed before they can be used.  One
>advantage of the automounter is that if you want a file, it is there
>automatically.

AFS, which any respectable DECAthena shop will use anyway, solves
this problem.  One significant disadvantage of the automounter is that
it is a maintenance headache.  Completely out of character with the
Athena environment.


>5) su and rlogin don't work.

su works just fine for us.  rlogin is a bad idea whose time is long
past anyway.


>6) Normal unix mail and related enhancements (e.g. xbiff and the emacs
