In article <3oe6vi$17l@fizban.solace.mh.se>, "Mikael �hrberg" <warrakkk@medio.mh.sd> writes...
>Ok, I have this weird idea that I want to convert a TU 81 PLUS to 
>an ordinary audio tape player.

Well, it would never be "ordinary", but if you like nonaphonic
(9-channel) sound, it just may be worthwhile...

> That would be really cool, but I 
>need some information about the TU 81 PLUS to even determine if
>it is possible or not... Is it possible to disconnect the logic
>circuits but still keep the BIASing curcuits?

It's gonna be hard.  The integral formatter in the TU81 will ordinarily
insist on a valid 1600/6250 bpi digital signal from the head in order to keep
the tape under motion.  (But there are TU81 maintenance modes that will induce
tape motion without requiring a digital signal to be read back from
the read head.)  And without the logic, you won't have any tension
and tape speed regulation.  If I were to attempt such a thing,
I'd probably start off with a simple older unformatted transport
(Kennedy 9000,9100, or the like) that doesn't have an integral formatter
and a complex tensioning/speed control subsytem.

> What bandwidth can
>the head take? 

Well, the TU81 could du 6250 bpi at 75 ips.  (Maybe with the
right firmware, 100 ips.)  That means they'll have a bandwidth
of at least 50 kHz; I wouldn't be surprised if it was over
100 kHz.

>Is it possible to store the music as analogue
>signals, or is a sampled signal to prefer? 

1/2" computer tape is specially formulated for saturation
recording, meaning it'll be extremely nonlinear over the range
it was designed to be used over.  With some extremely careful
bias setting, analog recording might just produce some passable
results.  But it'd be hard to set up, and the required bias levels would
change greatly from batch to batch of tape.

>If I can store it
>as an analogue signal, what dynamic could I expect? Can I store
>frequencies up to 25 kHz, sampled or analogue?
>Gee, that's a lot of questions, but if there is anyone out there
>that knows, please let me know, I'd be very grateful.

Again, modifying the TU81 to be anything other than a 1600/6250
bpi digital drive will be exquisitely difficult.  Try to find yourself
an older machine, preferably without an integral formatter,
and you'll spend more of your time experimenting with recording
and less of your time trying to regulate tape motion and tension.

Tim. (shoppa@krl.caltech.edu)
Kellogg Radiation Lab, Caltech.



