In general, this isn't a good thing to do, because users are then
penalized when they upgrade to a new machine which has a different
serial number. Worse yet, locking to the internet number is even
worse, because it means you can't change move to another ethernet.
You should be willing to move software to a new machine for free if
you do this.

Anyway, the getsysinfo program with the (undocumented) HWRPB function
returns an 'rpbbuf'.

	  err = getsysinfo(GSI_GET_HWRPB, &rpbbuf, sizeof(rpbbuf));

that buffer, documented in /usr/include/machine/rpb.h contains the field...

    char   rpb_ssn[16];		/* 040: system serial num: 10 ascii chars */

I've never looked at this field to see if its' really the ID or not.



