In article <3oiql3$djg@nic.lth.se>, swmike@trurl.df.lth.se (Mikael Abrahamsson) writes:

>We have a VAX 8550 (and some other VAXes) that we normally use RA82 
>drives too. Now we were donated a Seagate Sabre 2.2 gig drive, that 
>identifies itself as a RA91 drive. We can connect it and all, but we get 
>random bad blocks on it (or at least so it seems).

See the manual pages for help on the command radisk.  You can't do a
format (at least according to the manual page), but it may be sufficient
to scan for bad blocks (-s).  Anything that comes up with forced error you
can clear with the -c option.  Once you have data on the disk you'll
want to pay more attention to FEs.  If you can't get a clear scan of
the disk you might want to consider whether it is worth it.

>
>Therefore I'd need a format program that also scans for bad blocks to use 
>with this drive.

Depending on the subsystem (KDB50 or HSC) you may be able format if
you have to.  I think the HSC formatter may be pretty easy to get to.
If the disk is on the a KDB50 you may need field service.

