|> > SPEC #'s and other benchmarks are a good starting point, but *real*
|> > applications make for a much better benchmark. :-)

Well, I've never read or touched the NDA, so I will speculate
freely.  Unless Intel did something deliberately very devious,
there ought to be some simple, obvious instruction-sequence
changes that would get much of the benefit.  Maybe not *all*
the benefit, but much of it.

Why not just run some codes which show big performance 
differences between the Pentium and 486 code, profile the
code on each architecture and compiler, find some 
likely-looking lines of code, and look at what differences
there are in the generated code.  Given the announced
capabilities of the Pentium, these ought to be things
like how to pack 128-bit chunks of instructions so that
they execute in the minimum number of cycles.  Obvious
optimizations might be trying to pack combinations of
address-generating, loads/stores, and FPU instructions.
If you pack them the same way as the Pentium-optimized
compiler does, you ought to get the same benefit.

Anybody (who hasn't read the NDA) tried this and come up
with anything useful?  I'm being naive, no doubt, but
this kind of thing has been puzzled out before (I won't
even call it reverse-engineering; more like imitate
success and see what happens...)


