Subject: Our experiences with Multia.
Date: 1995/05/09
Message-ID: <3onlcf$p46@Trex.IenD.wau.nl>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 102296385
distribution: world
organization: Wageningen Agricultural University
reply-to: goet@rcl.wau.nl ()
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


About a week ago we received our first (and probably last) Multia.
Contained in 3 boxes we found:
- a Multia box
- a keyboard
- a 17" monitor
- a manual describing a 21" monitor which didn't look like the 17".

After having installed the critter, and switched it on, I found myself
looking to a bright blue screen, where I was invited to choose options
from a menu. Not knowing exactly what to do or expect, I've been looking
around a while in the setup, changed the timezone (which seemed appropriate
and not to destructive), and tried to startup Windows NT.
This failed, because it missed a file.
After a few phone calls with a tech-person from the supplier, it appeared
that one of the environment variables was wrong. After having changed this,
WNT booted. The tech-person told me that when the Multia logo appeared I had
to type Ctrl-Alt-Del to be able to login. Unfortunately I had ordered a
Digital keyboard :-( 
But after connecting a PC-keyboard I was able to login with the username/pwd
the techie had supplied.
After looking around a little, I decided to follow a tutorial which I could
choose by pressing a button on the screen. After the second or third
window this application crashed.
After having tried to startup TCPIP (which didn't work, not to my surprise
by that time), I decided to add a new user under WNT. I managed to add a 
non-privileged user. I even could login. The bad news however was
that since then I can no longer login to the original privileged user.

So I was stuck with a machine where I didn't have a system or root account.
After reporting this to the already mentioned tech-person, it took a pretty
long time before he called back. But then he had indeed a solution:
WNT does not have a backdoor to get privileges back; but we could return the
machine, they would reinstall whatever was necessary to get the privileged
account back, and they would send us the machine back plus an invoice for the
work done. The rest of the day I've spend wondering who's crazy.

A colleague who has some experience with WNT concluded after having looked
around at the machine, that we probably got a used machine (demo?); he missed
some files, which explained why TCPIP was not running.

Should I have received any documentation or media?

So far my experiences.

Kees Goet
Agricultural University
Wageningen, The Netherlands

P.s: B.t.w Ctrl-Alt-Del on a PC-keyboard is Ctrl-Alt-Remove on a Digital
     keyboard





