:We have a minivax with a modem connected (9600 bps) that will
:allow PCs to log in on a selected basis. Dialing from thesame
:office always works, and from outside rarely works. We have
:tried at all hours of the night, so line noise should not be
:the problem. They say this worked fine before. Is there some sort
:of configuration of the port needed? Do we just plug it in?

  I will here assume that you have the same configuration on the local
  and the offsite PCs, including the same modems, etc.  If you are not
  sure about this, please [re]check this.

  If a local modem connection works and the remote connection does not,
  then you will need to work with the PBX vendor (if there is a PBX
  involved), and with the local telephone provider (PSTN) to determine
  where the fault lies.

  I would suggest trying one of the more telecom-oriented news groups
  for additional suggestions, as the VAX system would appear to have
  little direct connection (no pun intended) to the problem.  (Since
  the modem connection does work locally -- the VAX has no clue that
  the call is coming from a local phone or an offsite phone, only the
  modem, PBX and PSTN are involved in this path.)
  

