   home.  :)  Also many companies have a policy against software
   piracy.

hahahaha - you think 'professional' companies and corporations don't
pirate software? think again...  software piracy isn't limited to
the folks @ home w/ PCs and students @ school. Its unfortunately
all too pervasive throughout the industry. 

The whole issue of licensing and license mgrs is a real sticky one on
boxes like your typical workstations, servers, etc, because the volume
of sales vendors make to those markets are no where close to the PC
sales (orders of magnitude difference). Software companies in the PC
market have probably given up trying various 'locking' schemes because
they end up backfiring more often than not and there is always some
hacker that will spend the time to crack it anyways.  They hope to
make up in volume of sales what they might lose in some percentage of
illegal copies. 

Also, there is the issue of support - typically in the PC market, you
have a one-one correlation between the copy of the software and the
user, so its easier to tag one user to a package. In the unix/vms/server
market, you often have many users and one copy of the software (with
a multi-user license). How would you propose to keep these folks 'honest'
ie., not buying one copy, one support contract and letting everyone in the
organization use it. The same issue goes for networked PCs and packages
that are installed on a server - what is stopping 100 people from using
one licensed copy?? Either you charge an immense amount for the one
license and tell 'em its 'unlimited use' or you charge a small amount
per user and find some way to enforce the user limit...

There needs to be some fair way to not hassle the buyer/user, but
still allow the software vendor to  a fair profit. Otherwise we all go outa
business ...

-art stine
visix software



