keywords: VMS backup
reply-to: muswick@max.uh.cwru.edu
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


I have used vms backup for years for dir to dir copies.  I had done performance
tests in the past and found it works quite well.  However, I started 
performance testing on a 2100-200, 128Mb of memory, vms 6.1 and found some
really strange performance numbers.  I am hoping that someone might be
able to shed some light onto why, and possibly different sysgen/rms
settings to improve perfromance.

The operation I was performing was:

BACKUP disk1:[dir1...]*.*;* disk2:[dir1...]*.*;*

Disk1 is a RZ28 2.0GB, disk2 is a RZ26L 1.0GB both on the same imbedded 
SCSI controller.

The average file size is 475 blocks, actually are alternating 10 block and
~940 block files.  All files are stream, CR files.

Backup took 239 seconds to copy 303,000 blocks or ~650,000 bytes/second.  Not
real fast.  Since these are pure binary files, I changed the attributes to
fixed:512:none, but with no change in copy time.

Next I tried doing a backup to a saveset, using:

BACKUP disk1:[dir1...]*.*;* disk2:[dir1]saveset.sav/save

Backup now took 17.2 minutes (1034 seconds) or ~150,000 bytes per second.

That was discouraging.

Now I tried copy with:

COPY disk1:[dir1]*.*;* disk2:[dir1]*.*;*

Copy took 86 seconds to copy the 303,000 blocks or 1,785,000 bytes/second.
Huge difference.

Here is the other strange thing is that using backup to goto a DLT2700
(on the same SCSI controller, we have only one) in 20GB compressed mode using:

BACKUP disk1:[dir1...]*.*;* tape:dir1.sav/label=test

This only took 112 seconds or a transfer rate of 1,385,000 bytes/second.

So BACKUP itself is not the culprit, only disk to disk.

Any light on this subject would be appreciated.
All testing was done under no load.

