>pdchapin@news.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) asked:
>> What do I gain with DECAthena as opposed to just running NIS/NFS?

To which Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com  (at DEC) replied
>You get a central administration point for users, file services,...
(and he went on to list its pluses).

Paul, you might be better off asking what you _lose_ with Athena.
You lose the ability to ftp into a machine and get files from your
file store (although you can ftp into a machine that you are 
currently logged into, which is ok if you can remember which one that is,
or you can bypass the whole mess by using automount (maybe)); 
you lose the ability to read your mail after you've been logged on 
for more than 12 or 24 hours (one's ticket "expires" yielding the 
message "Intruder Alert" if you subsequently type "whoami").  

It's slow, too, which means you sit around waiting to log out,
among other things.

Our local Computing Centre just dropped Athena on its 20+ alpha 
workstations in preference for NIS after over a year of numerous and 
continuous user complaints.  The Centre felt that DEC's support in 
providing upgrades for Athena that could run under OSF/1 upgrades was 
just about non-existent.  This is a big problem because loads of software
packages that we use regularly will only run under OSF/1 V.2.0 or 
3.0 -- but Athena seems to be stuck at V 1.3.

All in all, a real nuisance.
-julii
