In article <950501162126@d-cell.ftp.com>, stev@lard.ftp.com writes:
> very few people buy computers and build their own software to run on
> them.  alot base their purchase decisions on what platform the
> package they need runs on. i would imagine it would be in digital's
> best interest to keep people writing code on their products. one way
> is to offer cheap/free compilers. 

In order to offer cheap/free compilers, the compilers are actually being paid
for by some other area.  As you state, very few people buy computers and
actually use a compiler.  Therefore, these people people (the majority) will be
paying for the compilers.  This is where things get very, very interesting.  As
an end user, I may not be willing to fund your development effort.  If two
vendors come to me with identical boxes but at different prices, and the lower-
priced box didn't include the compiler I don't need, I'd probably chose the
cheaper alternative.  Given this, the vendors are now willing to sell me the
box without a compiler.  The bottom line is you usually get what you pay for. 
If you want the compiler, be prepared to pay for it.  If Digital can sell me
more workstations because they're cheaper, then maybe you'll be willing to
develop more software for it since your potential market is larger.

