In article <3nriac$s50@jac.zko.dec.com>, gregg@eggoop.zko.dec.com (Ed Gregg) writes:
>
>> mount -r -t cdfs /dev/rz4c /cdrom
>> /dev/rz4c on /cdrom: No such device
>
>Make sure /dev/rz4c exists. If not use /dev/MAKEDEV.
>see %man MAKEDEV for details.
>
>--
>Ed Gregg
>Digital Equipment Corp
>OpenGL/NT development

No!!

I had this problem, and it's because when you install OSF/1 V3.2 (uh, 
sorry, Digital UNIX), the new kernel does not support the cdfs file system by
default.  All you have to do is add a line something like OPTIONS CDFS in your
kernel file, and rebuild your kernel.  OSF/1 will then know how to read your
CD.  Have a look at the System Managers Guide on the OSF/1 documentation CD for
details of how to do this.

It would be nice if Digital could include this option by default.  I guess
almost everyone has to go through this nause of rebuilding their kernel after
installing OSF/1 so that they can use their CD drive on cdfs format CDs.

