That's because they don't use PAKs. (Would I beat up Microsoft because
I use 500 unlicensed copies of Word?)

However, many people in the larger IBM PC market beat up vendors that
used dongles. Some software (AutoCAD, for instance) still uses them,
but Lotus doesn't write dongled software any more, as far as I know.
On the other hand, can you guarantee that every single copy of 1-2-3
has been paid for?

> In short: DEC,
> wake up.

At the moment, Digital ships you a CD every few months with a whole
bunch of software on it. If you want to try any of that software,
you can get a temporary PAK faxed to you that you type in ONCE and
lasts for a certain number of days. If you decide to purchase that
product, you then get a permanent PAK which you enter ONCE, and
forget about from then on. (This may not be true for everyone,
but it should be for most cases.) I don't think it's too much to
expect that someone capable of managing an OpenVMS or Digital UNIX
system should also be capable of typing in a PAK.

How do you propose that Digital wakes up? What better scheme would
